    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What to Do as the World Falls Apart: A Framework for Action"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-13 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. MJSullivan flagged this as a second key episode this session (alongside Frankly_139), 'as it provides an actionable framework for things you can do.' Recorded during the real 2026 Iran war/Strait of Hormuz crisis, Nate explicitly marks this as a turning point — after 20+ years of diagnosis, he shifts to a first-pass, explicitly labeled draft framework for action, organized as 4 levels: a personal foundation of inner work (nervous-system regulation, agency, grief work), 'Finding the Others' (trusted networks, shared vocabulary, scenario/shortfall planning), Six Fronts of intervention (Infrastructure & physical stock/flow, Poverty & displacement, Ecological interventions, Civic resilience & governance, Culture & meaning, Economic transition/commons/post-growth), and a Timeline axis of three overlapping, non-sequential phases (Phase A: the Stability Window; Phase B: Bend Not Break; Phase C: the Stable Attractor) that cut across all six fronts."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2026-03-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/132-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls-apart> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-132-Transcript-What-to-Do-as-the-World-Falls-Apart.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/TH8aHQ3Y6Zk> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox, tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan, tgs:Persona.SianSutherland,
        tgs:Persona.ZakStein, tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly_88 ('Snow, the Singularity, and Rocks in the River'), Frankly_65 ('And Then What, Using Wide Boundary Lenses'), and Frankly_98 ('10 Qualities That Could Change the Future') — real, confirmed URLs, direct sources for the 'Rocks in the River,' 'Islands of Coherence,' and 'Cultural Mitochondria' terms Hagens uses here, none built as Episode individuals yet. Audrey Tang (TGS #169) and Helena Norberg-Hodge (TGS #181) both real, confirmed, cited by name with direct episode links — neither built as a Persona in this pass. Peter Strack (TGS #166, 2000-Watt Society) and Alexis Zeigler (TGS #171, Living Energy Farm) both real, confirmed, cited — neither built. TWO items this note previously flagged as deferred are now resolved, both same day: Frankly_58 ('The Fellowship of the Ring, Bend Not Break') is now built as a real Episode individual and added to dct:references above; and the SIX FRONTS/30 SUBDOMAINS/THREE TIMELINE PHASES this episode introduces are now fully minted (tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction, tgs:InterventionFront, tgs:InterventionSubdomain, tgs:ResponsePhase — see interventionfronts.ttl and responsephases.ttl)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate frames this explicitly and repeatedly as a personal turning point, not just another episode — 'as much a personal rite of passage as it is a Frankly,' gestating for a decade and finally organized under the pressure of real, live world events (the Iran war, unfolding in real time as he records). A genuinely notable rhetorical move: Nate directly names the risk of his own prior 20 years of work — that continued diagnosis without a action framework becomes 'a category error' once the underlying diagnosis is broadly accepted — and explicitly declines to spend this episode on news updates about the Strait of Hormuz crisis itself, redirecting instead to what he calls the only proportionate conversation: what to actually do. The most philosophically pointed moment is Nate's direct, carefully-hedged engagement with anti-capitalist and decolonial critique: he states real sympathy for those values and concedes 'there's much in those critiques that's correct,' but argues explicitly that naming the system correctly is not itself a plan, and that waiting for total systemic transformation as a precondition for any other action means 'waiting forever' — arguing instead for parallel construction, building new structures while the old ones still stand. The Timeline axis is offered as the episode's own 'most important orienting idea' — explicitly not a sequence to complete and move past, but three genuinely overlapping conditions, with Phase C (the stable attractor) framed as impossible to reach by skipping the disruption of Phases A and B, only reachable by building through them. The episode closes on a real, personal reflection on leadership: Nate argues modern systems select for Dark Triad traits (already a real, built Concept in this graph) in leaders, creating adverse selection, and offers a genuinely counter-intuitive closing reframe — that a viewer's own reluctance to lead 'might be a good indication that you could be a good leader tomorrow.' He explicitly declines to resolve the framework's own open question of what to do, insisting that question 'can only be answered by us as individuals,' a real refusal to prescribe despite having just spent nearly an hour building a framework that could easily be read as prescriptive."@en ;
    .


