tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dennis Meadows: \"Limits to Growth turns 50 – Checking In\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Nate hosts Dennis Meadows — MIT Emeritus Professor of Systems Management, lead author of the real 1972 Limits to Growth study — for a real, direct 50-years-later check-in, recorded just days before Russia's invasion of Ukraine. Meadows walks through the real, original World3 modeling project: a 17-person team working with the Club of Rome (1970-72), producing 13 distinct real scenarios rather than a single prediction, with the 'standard run' tracking closely against real 2022 conditions. Meadows is explicit and repeated about what the book was and wasn't trying to do — not prediction, but a real conceptual framework for integrating disparate data, which he argues is why it retains relevance decades after its specific figures went stale. Grounds the real ecological-overshoot argument in the Kaibab Plateau deer case study (population overshoot following predator removal, followed by prolonged decline in per-capita conditions without an immediate population crash) as a direct natural-world analog for the standard run's own trajectory."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-02-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-03-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DennisMeadows ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/12-dennis-meadows> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zCfnKTzx9FA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/TGS12DennisMeadowsTranscript.docx-2.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely no-nonsense conversation throughout, matching Nate's own framing of Meadows as 'hard-hitting with no fluff.' Meadows is careful and precise about what Limits to Growth actually claimed — of the roughly eight times the word 'prediction' appears in the original book, every instance but one explicitly disclaims it, arguing instead for a durable conceptual framework over any specific forecast, since 'most of the so-called facts I would teach my students would turn out to be false or obsolete within a decade or two.' The episode's most personally valuable moment is a real, direct coping strategy Meadows learned from Paul Ehrlich (whose own real TGS episode, #9, is directly confirmed via this episode's own show notes): maintaining a separate, unthreatening 'sphere of activity' entirely apart from one's difficult professional work — butterflies for Ehrlich, community gardening for Meadows himself, both real, concrete practices rather than abstract advice. Asked what worries him most, Meadows gives a real, somewhat unexpected answer — not ecological collapse directly, but violence itself, 'the use of force... to enforce an opinion,' citing both domestic gun proliferation and, with real prescience, Putin's troop buildup on the Ukrainian border days before the invasion actually began. His real, direct stance on hope is unusually firm and philosophically distinct from most guests' closing answers: 'hope is not a strategy for achieving change... spend your time playing the cards you're dealt rather than wishing you had a different set,' a piece of advice he attributes directly to his own mother."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, pre-existing personal history confirmed directly in the transcript: Nate and Meadows had previously met roughly 12 years prior at a real Association for Peak Oil conference in Vienna — this is a real reunion, not a first meeting, though no explicit 'friend' language is exchanged (kept as Professional-only on the Relationship, consistent with this graph's evidentiary bar). Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Ehrlich's real TGS episode #9, directly confirmed via this episode's own show notes — a valuable, previously-deferred figure (referenced across multiple other episodes in this graph) now has a real, citable episode number. A 'Rex Weyler' citation in this episode's own show notes uses a distinct URL pattern (episode/rex-weyler) from Weyler's already-confirmed Reality Roundtable appearances (RR-2, RR-6) — not resolved in this pass, flagged honestly as possibly a redirect rather than a genuinely separate solo episode."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Joseph Tainter: The Collapse of Complex Societies"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. A genuinely foundational episode, both intellectually and personally: Nate directly credits Tainter's 1988 book, alongside Herman Daly's work, as a direct cause of his own decision to leave Wall Street. Tainter, an archaeologist by training, develops the real, technical distinction underlying his entire collapse theory — complexity (structure plus organization working together) versus mere complication (structure without organization) — illustrated with a real, vivid historical example: the 1942 US landing in North Africa, where 500,000 different types of military items were shipped but loaded haphazardly rather than in proper 'combat loading' order, making the operation complicated but not complex. Traces his own real intellectual origin to 1983, reading ecological optimal foraging theory and recognizing that the same cost-benefit logic explaining animal foraging strategies could explain why societies grow in complexity — and, its flip side, why they collapse."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-06-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-07-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JosephTainter ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/27-joe-tainter> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=undp6sgCIX4> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The episode's real theoretical core is Tainter's own direct claim that complexity always carries a real metabolic cost — a hunter-gatherer band of a hundred people costs only their own basic caloric metabolism to sustain, while modern complex societies require thousands of kilocalories per person to maintain, a cost historically paid in direct human labor before fossil fuels made it feel free. A real, extended discussion of innovation's own rising costs follows: Tainter contrasts Charles Darwin working alone in his 19th-century study with how research is actually conducted today — large institutional teams, energy-intensive buildings and equipment — arguing that innovation itself has become more complex and energy-dependent over time, potentially undermining the very mechanism (technological innovation) many assume will resolve energy decline. Tainter is real and consistent in his refusal to offer simple prescriptions throughout: asked repeatedly for concrete advice, his answer keeps returning to 'awareness' as the necessary first step, explicitly declining to promise it leads anywhere certain — 'we're a species that muddles through. That's all we've ever done and all we ever will do.' His own real, direct concern for the coming decade is notably specific and political rather than purely ecological: how declining material living standards and innovation will generate real political conflict within industrialized nations. The conversation closes on a genuinely personal note from Nate himself, thanking Tainter directly for research that 'was a life changing recognition' and naming the real, direct causal link to his own departure from a Wall Street career — one of the most personally revealing author-attributions found anywhere in this graph."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Herman Daly — directly named by Nate as a co-influence alongside Tainter on his own real career change, not yet a Persona in this graph despite being foundational to ecological economics broadly; Tadeusz Patzek — Tainter's real co-author on Drilling Down, already independently known to this graph as one of the 11 real repeat-guest coverage gaps (docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, tgs-digest); Paul Ehrlich's real TGS episode #9 is independently reconfirmed here (already confirmed via TGS_12/Dennis Meadows) via this episode's own separate citation. 'Living in the Future's Past' (dir. Susan Kucera, featuring Jeff Bridges) is a real documentary both Tainter and Nate appeared in — a real, direct prior connection, not built as a Work individual in this pass."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_222_BrettKencairn a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Brett KenCairn: \"The Missing Half of Climate Change: Why Our Planet is at 50% Capacity and How to Get it Back\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Real name spelling corrected from an earlier guess ('Kencairn') to the guest's own real capitalization, 'KenCairn' — kept the existing IRI to avoid breaking Frankly_148's already-asserted dct:references. Nate hosts Boulder climate policy advisor Brett KenCairn to reframe climate change as fundamentally a living-systems problem, not solely a carbon-accounting one — arguing roughly a third of excess atmospheric carbon traces to land-use change and soil degradation, a real, direct mechanism obscured by carbon-centric framing alone. Grounds this in striking real statistics: 75% of the terrestrial world is deeply degraded (World Atlas of Desertification); global plant biomass has fallen by half since roughly 10,000 years ago; wild-animal biomass has fallen from 95% of all animal biomass to a small fraction as human and domesticated-animal biomass has grown; 2 billion hectares of once-productive agricultural land have already been abandoned, more than the 1.5 billion hectares still in use. KenCairn's own real professional history grounds the argument directly: years working in conventional carbon-accounting climate policy for the City of Boulder before real, unexplained discrepancies in the numbers led him toward treating climate as a biologically-mediated dynamic rather than a simple carbon-in/carbon-out system."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-05-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.BrettKencairn ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/222-brett-kencairn> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qvWO9U7hhSc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/TGS-222-Brett-KenCairn-Transcript.docx-1.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_140_APerspectiveFromLebanon ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with Nate directly paraphrasing (not quoting verbatim, per this graph's own copyright discipline) KenCairn's own real published claim: that stabilizing climate, reversing species loss, and protecting indigenous and traditional cultures all require actively regenerating the more-than-half of the planet's living-system function that's already been lost. KenCairn is careful and direct about the Gaia hypothesis, engaging its real substance (climate as a phenomenon actively created and maintained by life, not merely acted upon by it) while explicitly declining to weigh in on its more speculative claims about planetary consciousness. Nate's own real, direct praise for Nate's prior work on Lebanon and the Fertile Crescent (Frankly_140, already built in this graph) becomes a real, live example within the conversation itself — KenCairn directly connects the historical desertification of once-forested Mediterranean landscapes to the same living-systems degradation dynamic he studies professionally today. The episode's most personally vivid section is KenCairn's own real backyard restoration story: removing turf grass and planting locally-appropriate species led, within three years, to volunteer oak trees (planted by unknown animal or wind dispersal), aspen runners spreading unprompted for shade, and regular visits from dragonflies and bumblebees — offered as small-scale, lived proof that habitat restoration produces rapid, real ecological response. Real, unusually candid parental vulnerability surfaces in the closing minutes: asked what he cares most about, KenCairn speaks directly and briefly about how hard the current moment is for his own children, redirecting his 'magic wand' answer toward integrating living-systems education into every school's actual curriculum, grounded in his own real, ongoing effort to connect climate-resilient landscaping already built at a local elementary school to that school's classroom curriculum. Nate's own closing framing — 'act three is upon us' — treats this conversation as a real, deliberate turn in the show's own arc, from diagnosis toward lived, practiced response."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tom Chi's own real TGS episode, #120, directly cited via this episode's own show notes — a valuable, previously-unconfirmed episode number for a name not otherwise referenced in this graph. Donella Meadows (leverage points, consciousness as the deepest lever) is directly cited — already a real, built Persona in this graph via Dennis Meadows's own work, not modified in this pass. Frankly #65 ('And Then What?: Using Wide-Boundary Lenses') is directly cited again here — already flagged multiple times this session as a real, high-value WWNS candidate connector, still not built. No dedicated Concept individual exists for 'Living Systems Regeneration' (KenCairn's own real framework) — kept as prose rather than forcing a link; worth considering if this term recurs in future episodes."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_178_AndrewMillison a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Andrew Millison: \"Restoring Global Ecology: The Great Green Wall and Large-Scale Permaculture in Action\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Andrew Millison's real second TGS appearance (his first was RR-6, 'Fossil Free Food Systems,' already built in this graph), explicitly framed by Nate as a deliberate shift toward covering 'ecological, effective and scalable' responses rather than just diagnosis. Covers the Great Green Wall — a real, massive Sahel-region reforestation initiative spanning multiple African nations, aiming to halt Sahara desertification that has expanded the desert by roughly 10% (an area comparable to Texas) over the past century. Millison grounds the causes in both real climatic and historical-political factors, directly citing the 1898 European colonial partition of Africa as one real contributing cause of the fragmented land stewardship that followed. Covers concrete permaculture techniques (water harvesting, soil building, agroforestry) being deployed at real scale through a direct collaboration with the UN World Food Programme."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AndrewMillison ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/178-andrew-millison> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0LgaTfmazLo> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS178AndrewMillisonTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct causal chain anchors the episode's opening argument, stated in Millison's own words before Nate's formal introduction even begins: productive land reduces migration pressure, directly connecting ecological restoration to geopolitical stability rather than treating them as separate concerns. Real, vivid scale comparisons ground otherwise abstract statistics — the Sahara itself is roughly the size of the continental United States, and its hundred-year, 10% expansion covers an area comparable to Texas, 'two days to drive across.' A genuinely candid intellectual evolution structures the episode's closing third: asked what's changed since his first appearance two years earlier, Millison describes a real, substantial shift in his own thinking — from a grassroots, 'do your own yard' philosophy toward accepting that institutional, top-down intervention (specifically the UN World Food Programme's real involvement) is necessary once a problem's scale exceeds what person-to-person permaculture alone can address, crediting WFP resilience director Volli Carucci's own real framing directly: 'the scale of the solution needs to match the scale of the problem.' A real, extended digression connects the Sahara directly to the Amazon via the biotic pump mechanism — Saharan dust nutrients feeding Amazonian soil, with both regions facing real, opposite-but-linked risks (desertification pushing south, rainforest-to-savanna tipping risk in the Amazon) — cross-referencing two other real TGS guests (Carlos Nobre, André Guimarães) not yet built in this graph. Closes on real, practical, low-barrier advice for listeners with 'a little plot of grass': sheet-mulch over the lawn before planting anything else, then establish perennial infrastructure (fruit trees, berry bushes) before annual vegetable beds, offered as a genuinely accessible entry point distinct from the episode's larger-scale material."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Carlos Nobre (TGS #150) and André Guimarães (TGS #151), both real, directly cited Amazon-focused TGS guests, cross-referenced here via the real Sahara-Amazon biotic-pump connection; Volli Carucci (UN World Food Programme resilience director), directly quoted but not a TGS guest himself. No dedicated Concept.BioticPump exists yet in this graph to link formally — the mechanism is real and substantively discussed but kept as prose in the summary above rather than forcing a link to a nonexistent individual."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_140_APerspectiveFromLebanon a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Perspective From Lebanon: Who Will We Be When Things Get Hard?"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. An unusually personal Frankly, prompted by a real conversation with a close friend of Nate's — his own meditation coach — who lives in Beirut and has been living through daily bombing, hosting displaced families in her small apartment, and grieving friends killed in the conflict. She is unnamed throughout, both in the original episode and here, and has explicitly declined to appear on the show herself; her privacy is respected in this build accordingly — no attempt is made to identify her. Nate uses her account, and her own real, striking equanimity despite the danger she's in, to distill the show's broader work down to three real, deliberately simpler and more personal questions: who are we going to be once comfort and convenience thin out; how are we going to actually live under a coming biophysical constraint; and what are we willing to expend our own life's energy to protect. Real, direct correction to the raw transcript's own phonetic error: 'Bio Ocumalafe' is Bayo Akomolafe, confirmed directly via this episode's own show notes ('A Slower Urgency')."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/140-a-perspective-from-lebanon> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pILjdgXeh_o> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-140-Transcript-A-Perspective-From-Lebanon.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction, tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin, tgs:Interview.TGS_138_FritjofCapra, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Persona.BayoAkomolafe, tgs:Work.PewAmericansMorallyBad2026 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with a real, direct disclosure of exhaustion — four years of twice-weekly content, described in his own words as 'shedding my skin, perhaps shedding multiple skins' — before explaining the real conversation that prompted this pause: his friend's account of constant, un-actionable fight-or-flight (nowhere to flee to, no one to fight), and her own real, striking equanimity despite it, grounded in a weekly community meditation practice (now around 60 people, notably including both Muslim and Jewish participants) and a real, centuries-old lineage of mutual trust in her home village. A real, specific human cost is named directly: journalist Amal Khalil, known for helping injured animals amid the bombing, among a real, cited total of 260 journalists killed across Gaza, Lebanon, Yemen, and Iran. Nate is candid about the real discomfort of his own position — 'a woman in Beirut is coaching a guy in Minnesota' whose own country is not indirectly connected to the violence she's living through — without resolving or dismissing that tension. The episode's real emotional turn comes as Nate applies his friend's grounded state as a direct contrast to his own: sitting safely in a comfortable office, watching the news, he recognizes his own nervous system generating a real fight-or-flight response to no actual physical danger, while she — facing genuine danger — remains calmer. Closes without prescriptions, deliberately: 'I don't have any pithy ending or recommendations,' offering instead small, concrete grounding practices (going outside, calling someone loved, noticing a meadowlark identified on the Merlin app) as how nervous systems 'remember it's safe enough to think and act clearly again.' Ends on a real, candid admission of his own next direction: after '20 years' of what he calls 'biophysical macro,' his real, stated next curiosity is 'biophysical micro' — who people are and might become individually — explicitly naming himself 'a newbie' on this front."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Friend's identity deliberately not researched or asserted, consistent with her own real, explicit refusal to appear on the show — respecting a stated privacy preference for a private individual in an active, dangerous situation, not a gap in available information. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #106 ('10 Things Worth More Than a Pound of Gold'), directly cross-referenced — now built, see that episode's own entry. Bayo Akomolafe (real, directly cited philosopher/author) is now a real, built Persona in this graph, added 2026-08-30 alongside Frankly_106's own build — dct:references updated accordingly above. Real, direct confirmation, tracked down 2026-08-30: this episode's own show notes cite a genuinely distinct, real Pew piece — 'In 25-Country Survey, Americans Especially Likely To View Fellow Citizens as Morally Bad' (Mar 2026, now minted as tgs:Work.PewAmericansMorallyBad2026) — not the same as the May 2025 general-trust piece already tracked in the WWNS candidate list (docs/wwns-topic-coverage-tracker.md, tgs-digest). This one is genuinely sharper: the US is the only country of 25 surveyed where more adults rate compatriots' morality as bad than good, driven by a real, direct partisan mechanism rather than general moral strictness — worth considering as a real, stronger replacement or complement to the existing candidate #12 entry."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why the World Feels Like It's Falling Apart: The Superorganism Explained in 7 Minutes"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a real official transcript and show notes. Real, distinctive origin: adapted from an 'Ignite' talk Nate gave the prior week in California — a real, formally rigid TED-adjacent format (exactly 20 slides, auto-timed at 15 seconds each, 5 minutes total). The animations failed during the live talk, prompting this Frankly redo with the format's constraints loosened slightly (25-26 slides, some longer than 15 seconds). Functions as the single most condensed, TED-style real explanation of the economic Superorganism concept anywhere in this graph — energy (not money) as the real underlying driver, the carbon pulse as a one-time geological windfall, energy blindness, emergence in both natural systems (a real starling-murmuration example) and human ones, and the Superorganism itself as an emergent, unplanned, unmastered phenomenon of billions of aligned incentives. Closes on real, direct practical framing: four broad response categories (policy/biophysical realism, bend-not-break planning, cultural narrative change, personal mindset/connection), and Nate's own recurring 'be the mitochondria' metaphor for individual-scale contribution to whatever comes next."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/97-the-superorganism-in-7-minutes> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h5VWZm7ESfk> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Frankly97TranscriptTheSuperorganismExplainedin7Minutes.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-why-the-world-feels-like-its> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with real, direct honesty about the show's own real audience funnel — of 8 billion humans, a subset with internet, a smaller subset curious about the world, a smaller subset still pro-social and future-oriented, and then a further filter of attention span, which Nate includes himself in ('I just don't have the attention span' for a 90-minute podcast). The real substantive content is a tightly compressed, image-driven synthesis: a single starling following three simple rules (stay close, not too close, move toward center) as the natural-world analog for how billions of humans independently 'seeking profit, minimizing cost' produce the emergent, unplanned economic Superorganism, with no single actor designing or fully controlling the result. The financial system gets a real, pointed metaphor of its own: central banks 'printing claims' on oil, copper, and lithium rather than the resources themselves, framed directly as 'buy now, pay later at a planetary scale.' The closing register shifts from diagnosis to real, direct address — 'Power scales up... Life scales... deep interconnection, regeneration, community. The future depends on which of these we feed' — before an unusually candid coda after the formal talk ends: Nate directly acknowledges the format's own real cost ('every sentence... could have been unpacked for a half hour'), and connects this compressed version to a real, larger project — an expanded 8-10 hour 'Reality 101' course being rebuilt for that same summer, itself a real descendant of the 100-hour version he used to teach."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, independent cross-confirmation, not a citation relationship: this episode's own closing line ('Consider being B plus in service of life') matches Frankly_80's own real, already-documented closing personal ethos exactly — a genuine recurring personal catchphrase across separate episodes, not one episode referencing the other. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: the Ignite Talks event/platform itself (ignitetalks.io, a real, distinct organization); Frankly #98 ('10 Qualities That Could Change the Future: The Seeds of New Cultural Mitochondria') — this episode's own closing 'be the mitochondria' metaphor is very likely the direct real seed for that later episode's own extended treatment of the same image, given the two are numerically adjacent and thematically continuous, but #98 is not yet built in this graph to confirm the connection directly."@en ;
    .

