tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hacking Human Attachment: The Loneliness Crisis, Cognitive Atrophy, and Other Personal Dangers of AI"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by returning guests Nora Bateson and Zak Stein to examine AI's effects at the individual, relational level rather than the usual economic/large-scale framing — how chatbots are designed to exploit attachment vulnerabilities, the early evidence of cognitive atrophy and eroded person-to-person connection this produces, and what a more embodied, deliberately human-to-human counterweight might look like."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-20> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/RR-20-Transcript-Hacking-Human-Attachment-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real division of labor between two friends who never disagree but consistently work from different registers — Zak Stein supplies the mechanistic explanation (mirror neurons, the attachment system versus the attentional system, Harlow's monkeys and operant conditioning, the historical throughline from B.F. Skinner to AI tutors), while Nora Bateson consistently reframes the same point in relational/felt terms (warm data, the missing 'rhythm of breath' in digital communication, the edge of the self). Neither treats this as competing frameworks; each explicitly builds on the other's point rather than substituting for it. The one moment resembling internal friction is Zak checking his own argument's edges rather than disagreeing with Nora — catching himself mid-critique to note that dismissing all technology outright is its own kind of naivety ('how far will you push your techno-pessimism'), and that the same critical tools used against chatbots could equally be pointed at real technological benefits (Novocaine, reliable garbage collection) he doesn't actually want to lose. Nate's own participation is unusually personal for an interview host — he narrates his own emotional arc in real time (a 'pit in my stomach' partway through, open shame about once mocking the Amish for opting out of exactly this risk, then explicit relief and hope by the close), and at one point the conversation is genuinely interrupted by an unplanned real-world moment — a garbage truck's noise triggering his own threat-detection reflex — which he and the guests fold directly into the discussion rather than treat as a distraction, since it illustrates the very embodied, evolved cognition the conversation argues chatbots are bypassing. The clearest moment of real-time concept-building: Nate offers 'wide boundary self-awareness' as his own gloss on Nora's father's old definition of sanity ('the ability to perceive your own epistemology'), and Nora accepts and extends it rather than correcting it."@en ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found via this episode's own real show notes but NOT built into this graph in this pass, deliberately deferred: Frankly #112 ('The Quadruple Bifurcation') and TGS #198 (Stacy Mitchell, antitrust) — both real cross-references, neither yet stubbed as an Episode individual, so no dct:references asserted for either. Real, well-evidenced NEW Concept candidates surfaced but not minted this pass: Attachment Theory (John Bowlby), Supernormal Stimuli (Niko Tinbergen — directly cited via Work.RealityBlind, already in this graph), Goodhart's Law (Charles Goodhart), and a real Persona candidate, Harry Harlow (of Harlow's monkeys, foundational attachment research) — all real, all independently verifiable, held for a follow-up pass rather than built unprompted."@en ;
    .

