# ADDED 2026-08-13 — full RR-24 bootstrap, sourced from a complete real
# transcript + real fetched show notes, built specifically as a test
# case for fleshing out the "antagonistic" side of GreatSimplification's
# own contrastsWith network (per MJSullivan's own framing).
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Fantasy of Space Colonization: The Spaceship We're Already On"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is rejoined by two ~20-year friends, astrophysicist Tom Murphy and eco-interventionist DJ White, to biophysically ground the resurgent techno-optimist enthusiasm for space mining and off-world colonization — the economic impossibility of asteroid mining, the physiological brutality of long-duration spaceflight, and the argument that space colonization functions as a convenient story letting humanity off the hook for damage being done at home. Closes by redirecting attention to Earth itself as the 'spaceship we're already on.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TomMurphy, tgs:Persona.DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-24> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/RR-24-Transcript-The-Fantasy-of-Space-Colonization.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment ;
    dct:references tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Even more uniformly convergent than RR-1 — Tom Murphy and DJ White, ~20-year friends, never actually disagree with each other anywhere in the transcript; they build on and extend each other's points throughout, arriving from two complementary but distinct disciplinary angles rather than debating one. Murphy supplies the hard biophysical case (asteroid-mining economics that work out to roughly $130,000/ounce for plain dirt; radiation doses that make a lifetime in space a near-guaranteed early cancer death; the rocket equation implying an asteroid-return mission would need several asteroids' worth of fuel), while White supplies the ecological/consciousness case (his own dolphin-cognition and Greenpeace background, arguing the 'real profound stuff' — minds we can already talk to and are actively driving extinct — is here on Earth, not hypothetically out there). Both independently and repeatedly frame techno-optimism as displaced religion (AI as new gods, uploading as immortality, Dyson spheres as cathedrals), not as a rival scientific claim worth adjudicating on the merits. The one place real friction appears is not between the two guests but against the absent, unnamed techno-optimist position itself, which Nate keeps them pointed at with pointed, sometimes deliberately provocative questions (invoking Matt Damon's potatoes in The Martian, asking directly why two of 'the smartest people' he knows hold a minority view). The episode closes on a register shift from technical to personal — White's story of comforting a dying baby humpback whale, Murphy's closing 'talk to animals' — both converging one final time on the same point: the wonder people project onto space is already available here, and is being destroyed."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built specifically as a test case for the 'antagonistic' side of this graph's own network, per MJSullivan's own framing — the episode's real content directly and repeatedly names techno-optimism as its target (Nate's own words: 'the resurgence of ambitions for space mining and space colonization amongst the leading techno optimist technologists of our age'), not inferred from tone. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: a possible THIRD Nate/DJ White co-authored book (Nate's own words call DJ 'co-writer of three books,' but only 2 are currently in this graph — Work.RealityBlind and 'The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century,' the latter itself not yet built as a Work individual either); TGS_18 (Tom Murphy's own solo episode), Frankly_22, and RR-11 ('Tales from the Carbon Pulse,' Tom+DJ together) — all real, all deferred."@en ;
    .

