tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_1_ElectricVehicles a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Electric Vehicles\": Arthur Berman, Simon Michaux & Pedro Prieto"@en ;
    skos:definition "The inaugural Reality Roundtable — Nate brings together 3 returning guests (petroleum geologist Art Berman, geometallurgist Simon Michaux, and solar/EROI analyst Pedro Prieto) to biophysically stress-test whether dramatically scaling electric vehicles is materially, economically, and infrastructurally achievable, or whether current EV enthusiasm is itself a form of energy blindness — covering mineral/mining constraints, grid capacity, real EROI figures, and whether EVs address the actual root problem or just substitute one narrow-boundary technology story for another."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-04-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman, tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux, tgs:Persona.PedroPrieto ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr01-berman-michaux-prieto> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/RR01BermanMichauxPrietoTranscript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_3_OilBestAndWorstOfFuels, tgs:Interview.TGS_44_DieselAndTheBarrelOfOil, tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil, tgs:Interview.TGS_7_JoshFarleyAppearance1, tgs:Interview.TGS_29_JoshFarleyAppearance2 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Largely a consensus-building conversation rather than a debate — the three guests, longtime friends and repeat TGS guests with overlapping biophysical/systems worldviews, converge on a shared thesis rather than argue against each other. Michaux opens with a detailed materials analysis showing that scaling EVs and renewables to meet stated EU 2030 targets would require multiples of known global reserves and annual production of key metals, even before accounting for grid capacity, hydrogen production, or the fossil fuels still required to make steel, plastic, and concrete. Berman and Prieto largely corroborate and extend this from their own domains rather than push back — Berman explicitly says 'I agree with you, I provided you data to support your perspective,' and adds that oil companies' underinvestment is a financial-market choice, not a resource-scarcity problem; Prieto brings real Spanish/German grid-stress data and EV-affordability numbers. The closest thing to friction is clarifying, not adversarial — Berman and Prieto probe Michaux on his 28-day storage-buffer assumption and the electric/hydrogen vehicle split, and he candidly revises and defends specific figures under that questioning, at one point calling the underlying 'is this sensible?' question 'the best argument for degrowth.' The one moment of genuine, if mild, divergence comes in the closing 'how could you be wrong' round: Michaux allows an unforeseen technological breakthrough could upend his numbers; Prieto reframes his own uncertainty as being about timing rather than direction ('sooner than later the system will collapse'); and Berman draws a sharper line between his own specific technical claims (which he says could be wrong) and the biodiversity/ecosystem decline data underlying the whole discussion (which he considers not meaningfully disputable). All three land on the same practical conclusion Nate frames at the top: current EV scaling plans reflect 'energy blindness' — a narrow, supply-side technology story substituting for the harder question of reducing consumption."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Olivia Lazard (environmental peacemaker/mediator) was originally scheduled but couldn't attend due to travel issues, per Nate's own real substack write-up — deliberately NOT modeled as thinkr:hasGuest despite appearing in the show notes' own cross-reference list, since she never actually appeared. Her own real prior TGS episode is a separate, deferred candidate. Also deferred: Simon Michaux's own 2 solo TGS episodes (#19, #49, real URLs confirmed); Charles A. S. Hall (Pedro Prieto's real co-author on 'Spain's Photovoltaic Revolution,' already indirectly relevant to this graph via Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple) and that book itself as a real Work — both real, neither built in this pass."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-2 "Deep(er) Ecology," sourced from a complete
# real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload,
# plus a direct fetch of the episode's own real page for the table).
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_2_DeeperEcology a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Deep(er) Ecology\": William Rees, Nora Bateson & Rex Weyler"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three friends — ecological economist William Rees (originator of Ecological Footprint Analysis), Nora Bateson, and Rex Weyler — for a first-principles examination of what ecology actually means beneath its textbook definition (emergence, self-organization, dissipative structures, evolution), why humanity's own outsized ecological footprint makes contraction biophysically inevitable rather than optional, and what an ecologically literate civilization might look like on the other side of it."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-06-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.WilliamRees, tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.RexWeyler ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr02-bateson-rees-weyler> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/BatesonReesWeyler.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Overshoot, tgs:Concept.EcologicalFootprint, tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: William Rees's own real prior TGS solo episode (confirmed via a YouTube link in this episode's own real show notes, https://youtu.be/LQTuDttP2Yg, no canonical TGS-site URL given). The direct citation at 56:52 ('contraction of the human species is inevitable') links straight to Club of Rome's own real Limits to Growth publication page — the exact same URL already used as Concept.LimitsToGrowth's own dcterms:source, independently corroborating that source's quality."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The most substantively adversarial of the built RRs so far, though still warmly collegial — Nate's own opening explicitly names the contrast: 'people agree on deep ecology and the ecological predicament of human overshoot, yet take wildly different perspectives in how they view it.' Rees supplies the systems-scientific backbone (dissipative structures, thermodynamics, the mathematics of overshoot-and-crash), Weyler supplies historical/movement context and a harder moral edge (open frustration that environmental groups avoid words like 'overshoot'), and Bateson consistently redirects both toward relational process over prescription ('who can you be when you're with me' versus any engineered solution). The clearest real disagreement in any built RR happens here: when Rees says he'd prefer 1-2 billion people living well to 10 billion living in poverty, Nate directly pushes back ('I actually disagree... I think that's the default that we're headed towards'), and Rees concedes the practical point while holding his normative preference ('I agree it's easier, but I said IF you want to plan for something'). Bateson explicitly declines to fully endorse the contraction-is-inevitable framing Rees and Weyler share, prompting Nate to ask her directly whether she agrees — a rare moment where a built RR's host names a real, unresolved difference among guests rather than treating the conversation as settled consensus. Population and eugenics form the other real point of productive friction: Weyler raises population decline directly and names the taboo around it, Bateson immediately reframes the same territory through care and relational support rather than numbers, explicitly naming the eugenics risk in framing it any other way — not a rebuttal of Weyler's point, but a genuine redirection of its terms that he doesn't contest."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-3 "Unlearning Economics," sourced from a
# complete real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own
# upload, plus a direct fetch of the episode's own real page for the
# table).
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_3_UnlearningEconomics a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Unlearning Economics\": Jon Erickson, Josh Farley, Steve Keen, and Kate Raworth"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together four heterodox economists — two of them his own real PhD advisors, Jon Erickson and Josh Farley — each given roughly 6 minutes to name one core assumption of mainstream Econ 101 they consider fundamentally broken: Erickson on Homo economicus and the rational-actor model, Keen on energy's near-total absence from macroeconomic theory, Farley on the assumption that markets efficiently allocate essential resources, and Raworth on GDP-centric growth as the discipline's implicit endpoint. The stated goal is blunt: roughly 235 million university students worldwide are being taught a model of the economy that doesn't track physical reality, and the episode is explicitly framed as something to show in classrooms."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-07-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JonErickson, tgs:Persona.JoshFarley, tgs:Persona.SteveKeen, tgs:Persona.KateRaworth ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr03-erickson-farley-raworth-keen> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/RR03EricksonFarleyRaworthKeenTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: all 4 guests' own real prior TGS solo episodes are now confirmed with real URLs (Erickson #45, Keen #30, both via RR-3's own real show notes; Raworth's via YouTube, no canonical TGS-site URL). Real, specific named academic references that could become their own citation-only Personas if this thread is revisited: Elinor Ostrom (Nobel laureate, commons governance), Thorstein Veblen, E.O. Wilson (Consilience) — none built here."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The most substantively adversarial-yet-collegial of the built RRs — the only one where two different guests explicitly announce they're playing 'devil's advocate' against a co-panelist's argument, not just adding texture. Raworth does it twice: first pressing Erickson on what actually replaces the rational-actor model once you've torn it down ('then what?'), and later challenging Farley's rationing proposal directly, arguing markets plus redistribution and a universal basic income would work better than rationing given rationing's own real historical failure modes (black markets, Soviet-style central planning). Farley doesn't concede — he counters with a specific alternative (guaranteed access to essential goods specifically, not a general UBI, because essential-good prices are too volatile for a fixed income to track), and the two continue trading specifics rather than one yielding. Keen plays devil's advocate 'in a different direction,' agreeing entirely with Erickson's critique of neoclassical economics but pushing further — arguing the theory fails on its own internal mathematical terms even before you get to its empirical failures. The clearest real disagreement, though, isn't among the guests but in how each answers Nate's own closing question — can any of this actually be taught at scale? Keen is bluntly pessimistic ('I don't think they can'), Farley says he 'sadly agrees a little bit' but still points to concrete, working programs, and Raworth explicitly frames herself as 'leaning into possibility,' citing a real curriculum win (the Doughnut now appearing in an International Baccalaureate economics textbook) as evidence change is already happening, however slowly. All four converge without disagreement on Raworth's own closing 'Embedded Economy' diagram — Erickson calls it 'not rocket science' as a genuine compliment, and Raworth explicitly welcomes that as the whole point."@en ;
    .

