# ADDED 2026-08-13 — RR-4 "Titanic Oceans," sourced from a complete
# real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload,
# plus a direct fetch of the episode's own real page for the table).
#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_4_TitanicOceans a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Titanic Oceans\": Daniel Pauly, Antonio Turiel, Peter Ward"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together three real friends of his — fisheries scientist Daniel Pauly, physical oceanographer Antonio Turiel, and paleobiologist Peter Ward — who have never met each other before this recording, to examine the ocean's overlooked role in the unfolding predicament: industrial overfishing sustained by subsidies, the physical mechanics and real risk of AMOC slowdown or collapse, and deep-time paleontological analogs (Canfield oceans, the Permian and PETM mass extinctions) for what a fully anoxic, stratified ocean would mean. Closes on a direct, emotionally heavy question submitted in writing by DJ White (unable to attend as originally planned) about cetacean extinction."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-07-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielPauly, tgs:Persona.AntonioTuriel, tgs:Persona.PeterWard ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr04-pauly-turiel-ward> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/RR04PaulyTurielWard.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:EarthSystemComponent.AMOC ;
    skos:scopeNote "DJ White was originally invited as a fourth panelist but could not attend; Nate reads his own written question aloud instead — DJ White is already a real, built Persona in this graph (from Frankly_20/RR-24 work). This transcript independently reconfirms, a second time this session, that DJ White 'co-wrote three books' with Nate — the same real, still-unresolved detail first flagged during the RR-10 build (this graph currently only has 2 of the 3: Work.RealityBlind and 'The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century,' the latter itself not yet built as a Work individual). This episode's own real show notes independently confirm DJ White's own real prior TGS episode: #51. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: all 3 guests' own real prior TGS solo episodes (Pauly #15, Turiel #65, Ward #8)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Uniformly convergent and unusually warm — three genuine friends of Nate's, each bringing a different real disciplinary lens (Pauly's fisheries/subsidy economics, Turiel's physical oceanography, Ward's deep-time paleobiology) that consistently reinforce rather than compete with each other. The clearest human moment comes early and unscripted: Pauly, midway through the conversation, suddenly realizes he's been citing Ward's own book for years without ever having met him ('I said that I don't know Peter Ward, but I was wrong. I use your book all the time... I just didn't connect at all') — a small-world moment neither anticipated. Ward's own 'Medea Hypothesis' (life as structurally self-destructive, an explicit rebuttal to James Lovelock's Gaia Hypothesis) becomes the interpretive frame the whole conversation converges toward by its second half, culminating in Ward's own direct line: 'This is Medea. This is not Gaia... This is life doing it to itself.' The episode's emotional center is its ending: Nate reads aloud, in full, a written question DJ White had prepared before being unable to attend as originally planned — about the likely extinction of most large-brained cetacean species within this century — and all three guests respond with real, unrehearsed weight rather than analytical distance, Pauly describing starving, rib-visible dolphins in the Mediterranean he 'never thought I would see.' Nate's own closing words name the tone directly: 'human vulnerability and honesty,' and his reluctance to end the call is itself part of the episode's real content, not just a sign-off."@en ;
    .

