# fetch of the episode's own real page for the table). Resolves a
# real, repeatedly-flagged deferred reference — Francis Weller's real
# prior TGS episode was independently cited during this session's
# RR-17, RR-27, and Frankly_132 builds, all without being built until
# now.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Francis Weller – The Lost Art of Grieving: Grief as Ritual, Resistance, and Resilience"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts psychotherapist Francis Weller, originator of the real 'Five Gates of Grief' framework (from his own bestselling book, tgs:Work.WildEdgeOfSorrow), for an extended, genuinely deep conversation on grief as a suppressed but essential human and cultural need. Covers the real cultural and historical suppression of grief in the modern West (contrasted directly with grief practices in the Black Baptist community), the physiological and evolutionary roots of grief, individualism vs. communalism, and the real, direct application of ritual grief-processing to ecological loss (the ongoing 6th mass extinction, roughly 70% animal population decline) as much as personal loss."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.FrancisWeller ;
    dct:created "2025-01-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/167-francis-weller> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FrancisWellerTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bRne-E6iXKI> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.ArtsGriefAndSensemaking, tgs:Work.WildEdgeOfSorrow ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, notable coincidence worth noting, not a designed cross-reference: this episode's own show notes link the 'exosomatic energy' statistic directly to Reality Blind's own page 167 — the same number as this episode's own real episode number. Bill Plotkin's own real prior TGS episode, directly cross-referenced, was independently cited across 2 separate real formal notes this session (RR-17 and here) — no longer deferred, now built as a real Persona (see tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin) after MJSullivan's own direct instruction to mint him. Naomi Klein, Daniel Quinn ('The Great Forgetting'), Duane Elgin ('Choosing Earth' — his real blurb also appears on Weller's own book jacket, an independent cross-confirmation), and Jeannette Armstrong are all real, directly cited, none built. Real, direct thematic overlap with this graph's own RR-17 (John Seed and Skye Cielita Flor, also on grief/ritual) — not the same content, but a real, substantive resonance worth having both directions linked."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "An unusually long, deep conversation (over 3 hours) that nonetheless opens with real, immediate warmth despite being a first meeting — Nate discloses they share mutual friends and, discovered only off-camera, grew up in the same part of Wisconsin. Weller's own account of his path into grief work is genuinely personal: his father's major stroke when Weller was young ended his own childhood, forcing him into a caretaking role before he'd had the chance to be a child himself. The conversation's most striking moment of genuine, unscripted speculation comes when Nate directly asks whether leading a group of real Dark Triad-scoring individuals through communal grief ritual might soften their Machiavellian/narcissistic/psychopathic traits — Weller doesn't dismiss the idea, responding instead with his own real personal disclosure that it took him three separate rituals before he could shed his first tear, 'as a well packed white man.' A real, pointed counter-example structures much of the conversation's second half: Weller describes the Bohemian Grove's actual annual 'Cremation of Care' ritual — a real, documented gathering of wealthy, powerful men who ritually burn an effigy of care itself, offered as the direct photographic negative of the healthy grief ritual Weller advocates. Much of the conversation's final stretch is carried by an extended real telling of the Lindworm fairy tale, used as a sustained teaching device about immaturity, forced maturation, and what it actually takes (in the tale, scrubbing away seven layers of skin) to shed inherited armor. The episode closes with real, concrete, practical guidance rather than only philosophy: Weller's own suggested rituals that don't require other people (speaking a sorrow into a stone, burying it in the earth as an offering) alongside his real, pointed critique that people currently travel thousands of miles to reach his own grief circles, when the actual need is for ordinary local gatherings, not further concentration of expertise in one place."@en ;
    .

