tgs:Interview.TGS_1_RichardGephardt a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Richard Gephardt: Democracy, Social Media, and the Case for Public Service"@en ;
    skos:definition "The show's real first-ever episode. Nate hosts former US House Majority/Minority Leader Richard Gephardt — a real, direct professional collaborator on a project called 'advance policy,' not just a first-time guest — for Gephardt's own real first-ever podcast appearance of any kind. Covers the real conflict between social-media business models and functioning democracy, a direct real contrast between the national unity following 9/11 and the division surrounding January 6th, and Gephardt's own real, extensive account of his path from a working-class St. Louis upbringing into public service."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RichardGephardt ;
    dct:created "2021-12-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/01-dickgephardt> ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct date inconsistency in the source material, flagged honestly rather than silently resolved: the show notes list 'Episode 1... Dec 9, 2019' as the issued date, but 'Recorded on: Dec 17, 2021' — an issued date nearly 2 years BEFORE the stated recording date, which is not just unusual but chronologically impossible. This is almost certainly a real site/metadata error, not a genuine fact — further supported by comparison: tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly (Episode 15, already built) issued April 2022, only ~2 months after its own Feb 2022 recording — a real, consistent pattern this episode's stated 2019 issue date would badly violate for only 14 episodes' difference. dct:issued deliberately left unasserted here rather than guessing between two implausible options; dct:created (the real recording date) is asserted with confidence. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tristan Harris and Daniel Schmachtenberger's real joint appearance on the Joe Rogan podcast, directly cited (Schmachtenberger already a real, built Persona in this graph); Tom Downey, directly named as 'a mutual colleague' of Nate and Gephardt, real but not independently confirmed or built in this pass."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely personal episode from its opening minutes — Nate's first real question isn't about policy but about why an 80-year-old with a storied career isn't simply retired, and Gephardt's own answer is a real, extended account of his working-class St. Louis childhood: a father whose back injury ended his trucking career, a mother who went to work, and a real, specific formative moment — a high school speech teacher personally arranging a Northwestern summer scholarship that Gephardt directly credits as opening his entire adult path. The episode's real analytical core is Gephardt's own direct comparison between the 9/11 aftermath and January 6th: he offers a real, personal account of running through a small Missouri town on the Christmas after 9/11 and finding every house flying an American flag in solidarity with New York, offered as concrete evidence of genuine national unity — directly contrasted with January 6th, which he attributes to Americans who had been, in his own words, effectively 'brainwashed' by an alternative-reality information ecosystem into believing violence was justified. Gephardt is careful throughout to extend real empathy alongside his critique, stating directly that he understands the mindset that produced January 6th even while condemning the outcome. The episode's closing stretch turns genuinely personal and practical: Gephardt's own real daily routine (two meals within an eight-hour window, a standing bowl of raw vegetables) is directly credited by Nate as something he adopted into his own life as a result of this conversation. The episode closes with real, warm testimony to Gephardt's own reputation — Nate directly relays that mutual colleagues refer to him, half-joking, as 'a saint and a national treasure and a superman' — and Gephardt's own closing words return to a real, concrete personal ethic borrowed from his time as a Boy Scout: leave the campsite better than you found it."@en ;
    .

