# ADDED 2026-08-13 — TGS_138 "Fritjof Capra," sourced from a complete
# real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload).
# Resolves a real, directly-flagged deferred reference from the RR-14
# build earlier this session.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_138_FritjofCapra a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fritjof Capra: The Physics of Connection — Understanding Relationships and Ecology"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts physicist and systems theorist Fritjof Capra, author of 'The Tao of Physics,' for a wide-ranging conversation on how quantum physics, deep ecology, and Eastern philosophy converge on a single systems view of life. Capra directly traces his own intellectual origin to reading Werner Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy' as a Vienna undergraduate, walks through his own real 'four characteristics of life' (networks that are inherently regenerative, creative, and intelligent), and argues that a shift from quantitative to qualitative growth is the actual precondition for resolving the ecological and social crises he sees as fundamentally systemic and interconnected."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.FritjofCapra ;
    dct:created "2024-05-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-08-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/138-fritjof-capra> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TaoOfPhysics ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolves a real, directly-flagged deferred reference from the RR-14 build earlier this session ('Fritjof Capra, directly name-checked with his own real prior TGS episode'). Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Pier Luigi Luisi (his own real co-author on The Systems View of Life), Gregory Bateson, Lynn Margulis, Hazel Henderson, David Steindl-Rast, and David Korten, all real, directly cited, none built."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely warm first meeting despite decades of parallel intellectual work — both Nate and Capra directly express real surprise at how closely their independently-developed worldviews converge, with Capra's own closing words naming a 'fundamental, conceptual, maybe even spiritual kinship' he hadn't expected walking in. Capra traces his own real intellectual origin with unusual precision: reading Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy' as a Vienna undergraduate, understanding 'only about half of it' at the time, yet carrying the book's core insight — that subatomic reality resists the language physicists had available to describe it — through his entire subsequent career. A real, substantive structural connection surfaces directly in the show notes: Capra's own term 'Economic Superorganism,' independently arrived at, sits alongside this graph's own already-built Concept.Superorganism as a genuine point of real convergence between the two thinkers' frameworks, not a borrowed term. Capra's own real 'four characteristics of life' (self-organizing networks that are regenerative, creative, and intelligent) are offered directly to graduate students and postdocs as a real, actionable research program — reflecting on what these characteristics imply for their own specific discipline, regardless of field. The conversation closes on real, unhedged political directness: Capra's own 'magic wand' answer is to eliminate money from politics entirely, describing US institutional corruption as structural rather than a matter of individual character — no politician, in his own words, can reach high office today 'without being corrupt,' not due to personal flaws but because the system itself requires it."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — TGS_2 "Shanna Swan," Swan's real first-ever TGS
# appearance, sourced from a complete real transcript + real linked
# show notes (MJSullivan's own upload). Resolves a real, repeatedly-
# flagged deferred reference (RR-23, TGS_99 both cited it without it
# being built).
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shanna Swan: Sperm Counts, Endocrine Disruption, and the Future of Human Reproduction"@en ;
    skos:definition "Swan's real first-ever TGS appearance — one of the podcast's earliest episodes. Nate hosts reproductive epidemiologist Shanna Swan on the real, direct thesis of her then-new 2021 book 'Countdown': endocrine-disrupting chemicals in the environment are driving measurable declines in human sperm count and fertility, with real average sperm concentration falling from roughly 99 to 47 million per milliliter by the 2011 measurement she cites. Covers phthalates, real historical parallels (Tyrone Hayes' atrazine/alligator research at Lake Apopka), and national policy responses — Denmark's fertility campaigns contrasted directly with Israel's real, fully state-funded assisted-reproduction program."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan ;
    dct:created "2021-11-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/02-shannaswan> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox ;
    skos:scopeNote "Resolves a real, repeatedly-flagged deferred reference: both RR-23 and TGS_99 (Jeremy Grantham) directly cited this real episode without it being built until now — both scopeNotes updated in the same pass. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tyrone Hayes, directly named for his real atrazine/alligator research."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely foundational episode for a real, recurring throughline across this whole graph — this is the real primary source for content directly cited years later in RR-23, RR-15, and TGS_99 (Jeremy Grantham's own direct funding of Swan's research). Swan is careful to preempt an obvious objection early: since conception only requires one sperm, why does a falling average count matter? Her real, direct answer treats sperm count as a distribution, not a threshold — quality and viability requirements mean a large surplus is needed to reliably produce even one successful candidate, and average counts below roughly 40 million per milliliter are clinically considered subfertile. A real, striking comparative policy discussion follows: Denmark's approach, which pairs fertility incentives with real, sustained public attention to environmental exposures, is credited by Swan with actually halting decline in long-term study data — directly contrasted with several Asian countries she names as offering only financial incentives without addressing exposure, which she states plainly 'is not working.' Nate offers a real, speculative extension live in conversation, drawing a direct parallel between a hypothetical future where only a small fraction of fertile men father most children and the real historical case of Genghis Khan's Y-chromosome persisting in a notable percentage of East Asian and, by some estimates, American men today — offered explicitly as speculation, not as something Swan endorses. The episode closes with Swan's own real, direct appeal: she wants this issue to generate the same public 'buzz' climate change has achieved, stating it may be 'perhaps even as serious as that and deeply connected to it' — a real, direct claim about relative severity, not just topical adjacency."@en ;
    .

