# the extraction pipeline). Real, confirmed case of the audio-only
# "podcast_interview" category discussed this session — structurally
# an ordinary Interview (real host, real single guest, real speaker
# turns), just distributed without a YouTube video. thinkr:hasReplay
# used directly for the podcast MP3, per the decision NOT to add a
# 5th thinkr:EpisodeType value for a distribution-format fact rather
# than a conversational-structure one.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_34_KirilSokoloff a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kiril Sokoloff: \"What's the Most Important Question in Today's World?\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts veteran investor Kiril Sokoloff (Chairman and Founder, 13D Research & Strategy, since 1983) for a wide-ranging conversation taking a real, direct biophysical-economics view of global energy and financial systems — peak oil, the shale revolution, inflation as a real oil-induced phenomenon since 1973, wealth distribution, and geopolitics (BRICS, the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation, dollar hegemony), closing on Sokoloff's own real, personal reflections on Buddhism and what he calls 'the scaling of human consciousness.'"@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KirilSokoloff ;
    dct:created "2022-08-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/34-kiril-sokoloff> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/TGS34KirilSokoloffTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS34_KirillSokoloff.mp3> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    skos:scopeNote "No YouTube video exists for this episode — confirmed via the real scrape record's own explicit _gaps: [youtube_url] marker, not merely unfilled. Real, direct show-notes citations to this graph's own already-built Work.RealityBlind, at two separate specific page references (peak oil, and the real global oil-production decline rate absent new drilling) — a genuine, deliberate authorial choice to cite his own book mid-conversation, not incidental."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely wide-ranging, historically-grounded conversation, anchored throughout in Sokoloff's own real, direct expertise tracking multi-decade economic cycles. He offers a real, pointed distinction between deflationary and inflationary forces — technological productivity gains as inherently deflationary, set directly against energy depletion as inherently inflationary — and argues all US inflation since 1973 traces to oil. A real, substantive discussion of electric vehicles is notably unromantic: Sokoloff raises copper supply as a genuine bottleneck for battery production, not a solved problem. The conversation's geopolitical section is real and specific rather than abstract — BRICS expansion, the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation, dollar-based monetary hegemony, and Sokoloff's own direct, unhedged characterization of Western sanctions on Russia as economic self-harm for Europe. A genuinely personal turn closes the episode: Sokoloff moves from markets into Buddhism, the Dalai Lama, and Gandhi, framing 'empathic understanding' as a real precondition for navigating the systemic challenges discussed earlier — a real, direct bridge between his professional expertise and his own stated spiritual practice, not a tacked-on closing platitude."@en ;
    .

