tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 1): Desperately Seeking Agency"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-24 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. Opens against the backdrop of the real 2026 Iran war / Strait of Hormuz crisis (Operation Epic Fury), but Nate explicitly declines geopolitical analysis to launch a new, tentatively-titled 'Staying Human' series instead, opening Part 1 with the real question: why does growing awareness of the more-than-human predicament so often produce paralysis rather than action? He traces the gap through learned-helplessness and self-efficacy research (nervous systems learning whether effort produces outcomes), frames a digital environment designed to fragment attention as actively degrading follow-through, and reframes the Superorganism (already modeled in this graph) as a system that advertises choice while eroding the conditions for it. Closes with a concrete practice, not a program: protect one real hour in the next three days as a kept promise to rebuild self-trust — the precondition Nate argues is required before community-level and institutional work can follow."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    dct:created "2026-03-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/129-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-1> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-129-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ao2MJeiPiL4>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/0WdRQsyD7koPMV6i4tFNvS> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: three real cross-referenced episodes in this episode's own show notes — Frankly #61 ('The Strait of Hormuz and the Spice'), Frankly #127 ('Wide Boundary News 2/23/26'), and Frankly #98 ('10 Qualities That Could Change the Future: The Seeds of New Cultural Mitochondria', also cited by Frankly_132) — none built as Episode individuals yet. Real, well-evidenced NEW Concept candidates surfaced but not minted this pass: Learned Helplessness and Self-Efficacy (Bandura) — cited only in this episode so far, held pending recurrence elsewhere, unlike Concept.PersonalAgency which this series' own Part 2 directly builds on and cross-references."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 2): Navigating Dread and Carrying the Weight of Tomorrow"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-24 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. Opens on Art Berman's own framing of Operation Epic Fury as 'the biggest blunder in human history' (TGS_220), then turns inward: Nate names what he's actually feeling as dread, and draws on climate psychiatrist Lise Van Susteren's term 'pre-traumatic stress' (the body responding to a trauma that hasn't happened yet). Walks through the neuroscience in real depth — amygdala/HPA-axis/cortisol machinery evolved for immediate physical threats, chronically activated but never discharged by slow-moving abstract risk, producing allostatic load that measurably impairs the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex. Cites a real study finding ~28% of subjects are 'extreme dreaders' who'd rather take a stronger shock immediately than wait for a milder one — direct evidence dread itself can cost more than the feared event. Explicitly connects back to Part 1: 'agency is the opposite stance' to dread, since a meaningful action gives the threat system 'a different signal.' Closes with five practical pathways (mental reframing, somatic/breath practice, agency, community/co-regulation, and what Nate calls 'befriending the darkness') and five concrete steps for when dread arises, ending on a direct forward-pointer to Part 3's topic: the movement from dread into presence, and the default mode network."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    dct:created "2026-05-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/142-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-2> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-142-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-2.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4gJ9E233hfw>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/4lvZfG1sdQW7vnGZs8zc01> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-9e6> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency, tgs:Concept.Dread ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: TGS #202 (Samantha Sweetwater), cited alongside RR-17 as a Joanna Macy/Work That Reconnects cross-reference, not yet an Episode individual. Real Persona candidates cited by name but deliberately NOT bootstrapped this pass, matching this graph's established deferred-citation pattern (Audrey Tang, Helena Norberg-Hodge, etc. in Frankly_132's own scopeNote): Lise Van Susteren (climate psychiatrist, 'pre-traumatic stress' coinage, real personal detail — a childhood neighbor of Nate's), Viktor Frankl, Peter Levine (somatic experiencing), Daniel Siegel ('name it to tame it'), Joanna Macy (noted in the transcript as recently deceased), and Stephen Levine — none built as Persona individuals here. Francis Weller, cited alongside Macy, already exists in this graph as tgs:Persona.FrancisWeller — not re-flagged."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 3): The Default Mode Network and the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT 2026-08-24 from a complete real transcript and official show notes — no prior stub existed. Opens on a real personal anecdote (missing a dawn birdsong and a full cup of coffee while mentally simulating 2026 diesel-rationing scenarios) to introduce the default mode network (DMN) — the brain system active during self-referential processing, theory of mind, mind-wandering, and mental time travel, anti-correlated with the task-positive network (TPN). Cites a real Harvard study ('A Wandering Mind is an Unhappy Mind') and the real canonical DMN discovery paper, and explicitly points back to this graph's own tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie as the only prior TGS coverage of the DMN. Names 5 specific real reasons metacrisis-aware people are especially vulnerable to chronic DMN dominance (emotional loading, no resolution signal, social isolation, vocational necessity of simulation, and a moral license that frames presence as a kind of abandonment), and explicitly links this episode back to Part 2: 'the same phenomenon I called full dread in last week's Frankly, but viewed from a slightly different angle' — coining a new term, 'future capture,' for the DMN-dominance experience when the content is civilizational. Closes with five pathways back to presence (the senses, the pause, single-tasking, beauty, and the finitude of things) and three concrete daily practices."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    dct:created "2026-05-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/143-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-143-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-3.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fZB6Ep8qSos>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/3DzB8zatmpgek0Lfzkz67M> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-e54> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.Dread, tgs:Concept.Presence, tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork, tgs:Concept.FutureCapture ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain, tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist ;
    skos:scopeNote """'Supernormal' appears once in this episode's own transcript ('our supernormal, unbelievable modern era') but as a casual adjective, not an invocation of tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli as a term of art — that Concept isn't yet built in this graph, and this single non-technical usage is deliberately not minting it; the show notes' own link to it is show-notes editorializing, not something Nate himself cites here.
        Task Positive Network (TPN) is real and load-bearing to this episode's own DMN/TPN anti-correlation framing but kept out as a separate Concept this pass — folded into Concept.DefaultModeNetwork's own definition instead, matching this graph's 'don't over-split a paired mechanism' discipline. Real Persona candidates cited but not bootstrapped: Thich Nhat Hanh.
        Iain McGilchrist and his 2 prior TGS appearances (#85, #165) already existed in this graph — linked, not rebuilt."""@en ;
    .
tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman a thinkr:Series, dcmitype:Collection, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    dct:hasPart 
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, 
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2, 
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "MINTED 2026-08-24, resolving the gap flagged in Frankly_142's own scopeNote since 2026-08-13. Tentatively titled by Nate himself ('staying Human tentatively the title' — Frankly_129's own opening). 3 parts confirmed via direct transcript evidence of forward/backward referencing across all three: Part 1 (Agency) → Part 2 explicitly builds on Part 1 ('I spent the first installment of this series on reclaiming agency') and forward-points to Part 3 ('part three... will pick up soon on presence and the default mode network') → Part 3 explicitly builds on Part 2 ('the same phenomenon I called full dread in last week's Frankly'). No confirmed Part 4 as of this pass — Nate's own words in Part 1 ('more soon in this series') leave it open-ended; revisit dct:hasPart if a Part 4 surfaces."@en ;
    .

# added 2026-08-25

tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ed Conway: \"Material World: The Key Resources Underpinning Modern Economies\""@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT 2026-08-24 from the full real transcript and official show notes — no prior stub existed. Sky News economics journalist Ed Conway walks through his book's six foundational materials (sand, salt, iron, copper, oil, lithium), opening from the same well ('I, Pencil' — no single person understands a whole supply chain) that inspired the book after he stood at the lip of a Nevada gold mine and realized he'd never grasped the physical reality behind 'a hangover from empire' UK gold-trading statistics. Central, recurring thesis, stated in his own words at the open and close: 'how can we begin to fathom the future if we don't understand the present... we need to understand the basics before we even get to the complex stuff.' Builds this through concrete, real case studies of supply-chain fragility: the 1915 UK 'Glass Famine' (Britain let its glass industry wither, then discovered wartime binoculars were existentially dependent on optical glass, and had to trade rubber to Germany — its own wartime enemy — for Zeiss binoculars); and a single small Birmingham metal-pressing factory that manufactures roughly half the world's automotive rearview-mirror auto-dimming electrodes, an invisible real 'pinch point' Conway explicitly generalizes: 'multiply that by a thousand, a million... it is so complex you can barely get your head around it.' Also covers declining ore grades (US copper ore fell from ~40% concentration in 19th-century Montana to ~0.4% today, meaning exponentially more energy/water/trucks per ton of usable metal), a direct, real critique of net-zero transition models (the IEA's own models implicitly assume Sub-Saharan Africa's per-capita energy consumption stays flat through 2050 — 'in other words, zero development' — to make the arithmetic work), and an extended discussion of Jevons Paradox (already modeled in this graph) via LED lighting and AI/data-center energy demand as live examples. Closes on real, direct consumption/growth reflection: Conway states plainly 'the amount of waste and overconsumption in our lives is crazy,' and that writing the book changed his own consumption behavior."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.EdConway ;
    dct:created "2024-05-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-06-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EROIAndEnergyQuality, tgs:Subject.MonetarySystemAndGrowth ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/127-ed-conway> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/TGS127EdConwayTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4C2-tWcFKfQ> ;
    dct:source tgs:Work.MaterialWorld ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_167_OliviaLazard, tgs:Interview.TGS_SimonMichaux ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Warm and substantively convergent throughout, with Conway repeatedly building on and validating Nate's framing rather than contesting it — but not without two real, specific moments of honest divergence worth naming rather than smoothing over. The clearest point of genuine, if gentle, difference in register: near the close, Conway explicitly and self-consciously pivots away from the show's usual diagnostic mode, naming it directly — 'so much of the way that this discourse has happened in recent years has been about fear and about threat, but there's wonder too' — offered not as a rebuttal of anything Nate said, but as a real, distinct emphasis Conway clearly wanted on the record, illustrated with his own genuine enthusiasm for extreme ultraviolet lithography and the rediscovery of Roman concrete's recipe. The second real moment of honest non-convergence: when Nate directly asks whether excessive consumption stems from human nature or the economic system, Conway doesn't confidently pick a side — 'I don't know. I hope it's the economic system' — a real, disclosed uncertainty, distinct from Nate's own more assured 'I happen to agree' framing of the same question earlier in the conversation. Where the two do converge, it's substantive rather than superficial: Conway's own direct, unprompted validation of Nate's skeptical framing of net-zero modeling ('The answer is no, they don't [account for those constraints]') is offered as agreement from Conway's own separate reporting and interviews with policymakers, not deference to his host. The episode's real emotional through-line, stated by both speakers independently rather than scripted: understanding the physical reality behind consumption changes behavior — Conway discloses his own reduced gadget-buying since researching the book, and Nate's closing formulation ('understanding leads to appreciation, appreciation leads to gratitude and conversations and behavior change') is offered as a direct synthesis of what Conway had just described about himself, not an unrelated closing platitude."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode was specifically selected — MJSullivan's own words — as a 'general purpose response/caveat to pretty much any news article that talks about unlimited growth or consumption,' since Conway's real material-complexity argument applies far beyond energy/climate topics specifically. Directly relevant to the tng-app design work this same session: this episode is close to a canonical source for that app's 'systemic reframing' step (an article with no explicit energy/growth content that nonetheless connects to consumption/complexity patterns). Real, deferred cross-references found but not built: Olivia Lazard and Simon Michaux, both cited directly by Nate in his own intro as prior TGS guests on materials-for-renewables — their real episode IRIs are guessed above (`TGS_167_OliviaLazard`, `TGS_SimonMichaux`) and MUST be verified against the real repo before use; not confirmed to exist in this pass. No Persona bootstrapped for either here. 'I, Pencil' (Leonard Read essay) and Richard Baldwin (economist studying supply-chain 'nodal relationships,' a real, thin academic field per Conway's own account) are real, well-evidenced citations not built as individuals this pass."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_198_StacyMitchell a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Challenging Monopoly Power: Why Local Business is Better for People, the Planet, and Your Wallet"@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.StacyMitchell ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/198-stacy-mitchell> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ohwaf1N94sM>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/1SLnqgoUGC84mZabc2BtgI> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/TGS-198-Stacy-Mitchell-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Stacy Mitchell (Institute for Local Self-Reliance) argues that concentrated corporate power operates as a de facto governing force — deciding which communities thrive and which don't — not through superior performance but through structural market power ('strong arming the system'). She grounds this in real, concrete supply-chain fragility examples: a single slaughterhouse failure emptying meat shelves nationwide during COVID, one baby-formula plant failure causing a national shortage. Nate connects this directly to his own economic-Superorganism framing, comparing the system to 'the Borg.' The conversation's most consequential turn links economic powerlessness directly to democratic erosion: Mitchell argues that as communities lose local control over decisions affecting their own wellbeing, the resulting powerlessness becomes 'poison in the veins of democracy,' driving people toward strongman politics and, in the most severe cases, political violence — a real, direct causal mechanism, not just correlation. The episode closes on food deserts specifically (nearly 40 million Americans lack nearby grocery access) as a concrete, human-scale entry point into the otherwise abstract monopoly-power conversation." ;
    skos:scopeNote "Previously identified as a real, valuable cross-reference during an earlier episode-minting pass (see this graph's own note on the episode that first surfaced it) but deliberately deferred rather than stubbed prematurely. Built at MJSullivan's explicit request. CORRECTED 2026-08-29: an earlier draft of this entry asserted dct:references directly from this episode to four Work individuals (Deseret farm-bailout, World Bank food security, Pew declining trust, Carbon Majors) — but this episode never actually cites any of them; that link was standing in for 'candidate WWNS pairing,' a downstream editorial judgment, not a real citation relationship the episode itself carries. MJSullivan caught this: the correct shape is Episode -> WWNSArticle -> Work, not Episode -> Work directly, matching how tgs:WWNSArticle.WWNS_1 already properly models its own real references. This episode is not currently referenced by any built WWNSArticle; the four Works above remain real, valid candidates for a future piece, tracked outside this graph until one is actually written."@en ;
    .

# added 2026-08-29, from real transcripts + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
# Both surfaced as the real episodes WWNS #1 actually cites — resolving the
# earlier honest correction that TGS_198 (Stacy Mitchell) was never actually
# used in that piece, despite being a plausible structural candidate.

tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis: The Rules of the Game Keep Us Trapped"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts systems theorist Greg Elliott, author of the Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis (PSH), to examine dark triad traits (psychopathy, Machiavellianism, narcissism) at an institutional/civilizational scale rather than an individual one — explicitly framed as a direct follow-up to RR-19 (Nancy McWilliams and Reid Meloy), which addressed the same traits at the individual level. Elliott argues game-theoretic dynamics and increasing societal scale gave real historical advantage to instrumental/exploitative behavioral profiles, especially during the agricultural revolution and again with fossil fuel expansion — and that institutions, once captured by these dynamics, change the rules of the game itself, raising the cost of staying pro-social. Draws on Steinbeck's 'The Grapes of Wrath' as a direct literary analogy: 'the bank is something more than men... it's the monster' — institutional behavior detaching from the values and intentions of the individuals inside it."@en ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.GregElliott ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/231-greg-elliott> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gVlfwKUssPg> ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-26"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Elliott grounds the theory in real, direct personal experience — years working in financial markets, where he 'repeatedly encountered' behavioral profiles (emotional detachment, grandiosity, status-seeking, impression management) unusually well-adapted to that competitive environment, careful throughout not to claim clinical diagnosis. The conversation's real philosophical core is institutional detachment: individuals create institutions whose aggregate behavior can become divorced from the values, intentions, and norms of the people inside them — Elliott's own Steinbeck analogy makes this vivid rather than abstract. Real, direct personal connections surface throughout: Elliott describes becoming 'pretty close friends' with William Rees (already a real Persona in this graph) after Rees endorsed the book, and separately references a real connection with Zak Stein (also already built). The episode closes on an unusually personal, unguarded register: Elliott's own 'magic wand' answer is genuinely wistful rather than programmatic — wanting to time-travel and live as ancestors did — and his closing advice to young people (his own niece, nephew, and twin brother) centers on resisting technology-dependence and rebuilding community specifically because, in his own words, 'it's easier to dominate people who are atomized than it is to dominate groups of people.' Nate and Elliott exchange direct, mutual 'friend' language at the close — a real, first-episode instance of warmth this graph elsewhere only sees with repeat guests."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: 'The Grapes of Wrath' (Steinbeck) is a real, direct literary citation but not minted as a thinkr:Work — a classic novel used as analogy, not a TGS-relevant expert/research work in the sense this graph's Work class otherwise captures. Lisi Krall and David Sloan Wilson are both referenced indirectly (via Gowdy's TGS_14, not this episode directly) — see that episode's own scopeNote. dct:created deliberately left unasserted: one real source (YouTube video description) states a recording date of Aug 13, 2024 — over two years before the confirmed Aug 26, 2026 publish date, an unusually large and unconfirmed gap given the episode's own content reads as clearly contemporary (2026-era AI/game-theory framing). Flagged honestly rather than asserted as fact, matching this graph's established practice for unresolved date discrepancies (cf. TGS_9)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Interview.TGS_14_JohnGowdy a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "John Gowdy: \"Superorganisms, Crazy Ants, and Fire Apes, Oh My!\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts ecological economist John Gowdy — 'my friend and one of my intellectual mentors,' per Nate's own direct words, with a real, stated ~20-year prior relationship — to trace the evolutionary and economic origins of the human superorganism. Gowdy argues evolutionary biology has moved well beyond the 'selfish gene' model (epigenetics, group/multi-level selection, culturally-contingent cooperation — citing a real Chinese rice-vs-wheat farming study where rice-region cooperation norms measurably differ from wheat-region ones), and that human 'ultrasociality' (the same evolutionary category as ants and termites) emerged from the economic surplus dynamics of agriculture specifically, not fixed human nature. Covers capitalism as the latest manifestation of a much older growth/exploitation dynamic rather than its root cause, and closes on Gowdy's own real, direct concerns about US democratic backsliding and his hope that ongoing right-wing political fragmentation might take a constructive direction."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-02-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-04-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnGowdy ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/14-john-gowdy> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GgDIhhYKWU0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/TGS14JohnGowdyTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.Ultrasocial2021, tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Gowdy opens by dismantling the 'selfish gene' model as outdated, real evidence spanning epigenetics (a real Australian finch study showing maternal song altering offspring development by environmental condition) through a real Science-journal study finding measurably higher cooperation scores among people from rice-farming Chinese villages versus wheat-farming ones — direct evidence, in his telling, that human nature is culturally contingent on economic base, not fixed. The episode's real intellectual center is his own co-authored work (with Lisi Krall, not yet built in this graph) placing humans in the same 'ultrasocial' evolutionary category as ants and termites — a real, direct throughline to Nate's own Superorganism framing, which Gowdy engages with directly rather than merely validates: asked whether democracy is synonymous with an intact superorganism, he answers that the superorganism itself is not democratic, citing real historical material from Hayek and the Mont Pelerin Society explicitly discussing keeping populations under-educated and dependent as a stabilizing mechanism. Gowdy is careful and explicit that capitalism is 'not the deep underlying cause' of the dynamics discussed — 'just kind of the latest manifestation of growth and exploitation,' with economists' real institutional role being to protect the status quo rather than to describe it neutrally, in his own stated view. The conversation closes on real, personal territory: unprompted, live UN outreach during recording about his own real conservation work on South Sudan's Sudd wetland (the world's second-largest, after the Pantanal), threatened by a planned drainage project pushed by Egypt and China. Gowdy's own closing political concerns — stated directly as his own view, not asserted here as fact — center on US democratic backsliding, tempered by real, stated hope that ongoing fragmentation within the American right might eventually take a constructive turn. His closing practical advice echoes his own opening thesis: look past proximate causes (a specific policy, a specific villain) to ultimate ones — 'like the thing with ants, termites, and humans... it's these economic forces that really drove that outcome.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Lisi Krall (SUNY Cortland, Gowdy's real co-author on the ants/social-insects research underlying Ultrasocial); David Sloan Wilson — already flagged deferred elsewhere in this graph (RR-19's own scopeNote) — is independently re-confirmed here via this episode's own real show notes, strengthening rather than newly surfacing that deferred candidate. 'Nate's Superorganism paper' is directly cited in this episode's own show notes (a real ScienceDirect article) but not independently modeled as its own Work individual in this pass — deferred, matching this graph's practice of not over-minting Nate's own writing without a clear need."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-100, from a complete real transcript + show
# notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Genuinely special case: real, content-
# confirmed role reversal — Kate Raworth (host) interviews Nate Hagens
# (guest) for this one episode only, "turning the tables" for the
# show's 100th episode. Modeled with thinkr:hasHost/hasGuest reflecting
# the REAL roles for this specific episode, not the site's own generic
# metadata (which defaults every episode's "host" field to Nate
# regardless of content — confirmed by direct inspection of this
# episode's own real YAML scrape, which still lists host: Nate Hagens
# despite the transcript and description both explicitly describing
# the reversal). Also prompted a real cleanup this same pass: both
# tgs:Human.KateRaworth and tgs:Persona.KateRaworth had been
# accidentally split into two duplicate same-IRI definitions across
# earlier sessions — harmless under RDF's set semantics (both would
# merge on parse) but redundant; consolidated into one clean
# definition each, no content lost.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nate Hagens: \"Episode 100 – The Great Simplification\" (Interviewed by Kate Raworth)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The show's 100th episode, and a genuine role reversal: Kate Raworth interviews Nate Hagens, at his own explicit request ('I have asked for episode 100, a friend of mine who works in a related space to interview me'). Functions as a comprehensive, real synthesis of the show's own first two years — Kate runs Nate through rapid one-minute explanations of his own core framework (energy blindness, the carbon pulse, the economic Superorganism, the Mordor economy, the four horsemen, the metacrisis) before moving into deeper, more personal territory: financial overshoot and the 'bend or break' moment, degrowth vs. post-growth, the rural-vs-urban sustainability tension, pro-social prepping, and Nate's own account of how doing the show for two years has changed his thinking. Closes on real personal reflection — what he cares about most (the natural world, not humans specifically), what he hopes for, and his own 'magic wand' answer (helping people adopt a genuinely wider-boundary view of self)."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-11-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-12-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.KateRaworth ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/100-nate-hagens> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GocuMZX3hIs> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/TGS100NateHagensKateRaworthTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple,
        tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Structured deliberately as two real movements, both named by Kate herself: a fast 'speed round' of one-minute concept explanations (energy blindness, the carbon pulse, the Superorganism, the Mordor economy, the metacrisis, the four horsemen), followed by a slower, more personal second half. Nate is candid about the discomfort of being interviewed on his own show — 'I'm a little nervous' — and Kate is careful to name and hold that discomfort rather than rush past it, at one point pausing to acknowledge 'what it's like from the other side.' The real substantive core, per Kate's own framing, is the Mordor economy concept: energy's own real, rising share of GDP (roughly 5% in 1999, over 10% by this recording) as evidence that 'renewables will simply fix it' claims understate net-energy costs — she pushes directly on this with the fusion/AI-efficiency counter-argument, and Nate's answer engages Jevons Paradox head-on rather than deflecting. A real, extended, unresolved tension structures the middle third: Kate's own direct challenge that not everyone can 'go rural and buy land,' grounded in her own lived experience among UK climate-movement colleagues who have — Nate doesn't resolve it, offering 'pro-social prepping' (community and shared responsibility over individual land-buying) as a partial answer while explicitly admitting 'I don't know the answer to that' regarding the urban/rural question itself. The most philosophically direct exchange is Kate's own citation of a prior TGS guest, Robert Sapolsky ('we are hardwired to get it wrong'), pressing Nate on whether the Superorganism is genuinely beyond anyone's control — his answer is unhedged: 'the Superorganism is in control,' citing new Alaska and North Sea oil leases approved by ostensibly climate-aware leaders as live evidence, while still holding that changing 'initial conditions' before the system's own momentum breaks remains real, available work. The episode closes on unusually personal register even by this show's standards: audience-submitted questions Kate had crowdsourced on Twitter, Nate's own account of a screaming goat as a genuine nervous tic before hard questions, and a direct, unhedged answer about what he cares about most — not humans, but 'the natural world and other species that have no voice.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Selected by MJSullivan as the canonical reference episode for tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification itself — see that Concept's own updated scopeNote. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass, all already-flagged real gaps elsewhere in this graph: Robert Sapolsky's own real prior TGS episode (directly cross-referenced in this episode's own show notes), Jean-Marc Jancovici's and Helen Thompson's real prior TGS episodes (both already tracked as real 'repeat guest' gaps in docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, tgs-digest), and Joslin Faith Kehdy's real prior TGS episode (not yet flagged elsewhere). Kate Raworth's own real Doughnut Economics book/site is directly cited but not minted as a separate Work — SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics already captures this territory in this graph, referenced directly instead of duplicating it."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-30 — Frankly-071, from a complete real transcript + show
# notes uploaded by MJSullivan. High-value WWNS pipeline pick, flagged
# earlier this session as a real multi-candidate connector — real
# content REVISES that earlier guess rather than confirming it: the
# original citation-search-based assumption paired this episode with
# debt (#8) AND food security (#13), but the real transcript's actual
# supply-chain focus is general (tech/manufacturing, weaponized consumer
# electronics), not food/agriculture — food security never comes up.
# The real, better-supported pairing is debt (#8) AND declining trust
# (#12) — the show's own real show notes cite Pew Research directly,
# three separate times, on the exact trust theme candidate #12 needs.
