tgs:Monologue.Frankly_71_WeakestLinks a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Weakest Links: Depletion, Supply Chains, and Trust"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded within days of a real, live geopolitical event — the September 2024 Israeli pager/walkie-talkie attack on Hezbollah operatives in Lebanon — Nate treats it as a real, potential 'black swan' for one of his own Four Horsemen, then broadens out into seven things he sees declining largely out of public view: oil well productivity (citing a real, contemporary ExxonMobil report on a ~15% average annual decline rate), planetary-boundary stability, the value of money (real, specific federal debt-growth figures), institutional/interpersonal/media trust, civility, collective nervous-system regulation, and — the episode's real central focus — the stability of global just-in-time supply chains and globalization itself. Applies evolutionary game theory's cooperation/selfishness/altruism/spite framework directly to the pager attack, with 'spite' — 'doing something at a cost to yourself, but a greater cost to your competitor' — offered as Nate's own real, direct read of why Israel would choose this specific, costly, escalatory tactic."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-09-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/71-weakest-links> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=izxQcGnT6P4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Frankly71TranscriptWeakestLinks.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with real, disarming personal candor — recorded 'under the influence of Zizol,' an antihistamine for ragweed allergies, and admitting he's behind on Franklies with 'a list of 37 more' waiting. The real analytical spine is the seven-item decline list, delivered in Nate's own words as deliberately 'in no particular order' despite counting down from seven to one — oil well decline rates, planetary boundaries (explicitly naming six of the real, specific thresholds — biosphere integrity, novel entities like endocrine disruptors and plastics, land-system change, freshwater change, biogeochemical flows), the real value of money eroding under debt growth, declining trust, declining civility, nervous-system dysregulation, and finally supply-chain fragility as the real, live news-driven focus. The most direct, real personal reasoning comes in Nate's own account of why he thinks Israel chose this specific tactic — walking through the full game-theory payoff matrix (cooperation, selfishness, altruism, spite) live, landing on spite as the only framework that fits: a tactic that costs the actor real reputational and commercial damage (global South buyers now wary of Israeli, UK, and US supply-chain partners) specifically because it costs the target more. Nate is careful and explicit about the limits of his own analysis here — 'I don't even want to touch' the underlying Israel-Hezbollah conflict itself, keeping the discussion scoped to what it reveals about globalization's fragility rather than the conflict's own merits. Closes by directly connecting the whole discussion back to The Great Simplification and nearshoring, naming Russia as one of the only real countries with genuine end-to-end supply-chain oversight, and previewing a real forthcoming guest, Roman Krznaric."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "dct:references to TGS_100 confirmed directly, not inferred: this episode's own real show notes link 'Four Horsemen' to a specific timestamp (18:23) within TGS_100's own real YouTube video (youtube.com/watch?v=GocuMZX3hIs&t=1103s) — the exact same video ID already used as TGS_100's own thinkr:hasReplay. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: 'Frankly on spite' is real Frankly #11 (confirmed 2026-08-31 by MJSullivan directly), directly cited by URL, still not built; Roman Krznaric — already a real, independently-flagged deferred citation elsewhere in this graph (TGS_213's own scopeNote, via his real episode #142) — reconfirmed here, not a new find. Pew Research's own three real, distinct citations on institutions/interpersonal/media trust (all in this episode's own real show notes) are a strong, direct match for the WWNS candidate Pew declining-trust piece — noted for future WWNS pipeline use, not asserted as a formal reference here since no WWNS article has used this pairing yet."@en ;
    .

