#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_106_TenThingsWorthMoreThanAPoundOfGold a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "10 Things Worth More Than a Pound of Gold"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted by gold hitting a real all-time high the week before recording, Nate works through a real, structured list of ten things he argues are worth more than a pound of gold — moving deliberately from financial instruments (cash, silver, half a Bitcoin, all roughly gold's real market value at time of recording) through land, then into explicitly non-pecuniary territory: friends and social networks, freedom, sanity, integrity, the web of life, and finally life itself. Frames the whole exercise as a direct challenge to a culture that has 'become a microcosm of a monetary King Midas, turning the natural world into shiny representations of status.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/106-10-things-worth-more-than-a-pound-of-gold> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6wUntJ1OTmw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-106-Transcript-10-Things-Worth-More-Than-a-Pound-of-Gold.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_113_LynAlden ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely personal disclosure anchors the integrity section: Nate recounts being caught in a lie as a junior-high student, and his own father's direct response — 'your word is your bond' — which he admits sounded 'silly' at 14 but which he says he only fully understood '40 years later.' The financial items are treated with real, specific nuance rather than dismissed outright — Bitcoin's real dependence on continuous electricity and connectivity is named directly as a structural vulnerability gold doesn't share, and Nate credits Lyn Alden (already a real, built guest in this graph, TGS_113) directly for the framing that current fiscal and monetary trends mean 'there's no stopping this train' toward gold's own rising nominal value. Real, current data grounds the broader argument: the S&P 500 hitting a record high in the same week gold did, framed as evidence that 'the real stock market is crashing' once measured against actual planetary boundaries rather than currency — Nate cites six of nine planetary boundaries as already crossed, with a seventh (ocean acidification) close behind. The closing reframe distinguishes value from two other real, distinct properties — liquidity (how easily something converts to something else) and resilience (how well it holds up under stress) — landing on resilience, not liquidity, as what will actually matter 'through the storms ahead,' even though the things Nate names as most valuable (land, integrity, the web of life) are, in his own words, the least liquid of anything on the list."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: none of substance beyond what's already captured — this is a self-contained monologue with few external citable entities beyond Lyn Alden, already built."@en ;
    .

