# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-84 (Jean-Marc Jancovici), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Closes one of the 11
# original real repeat-guest coverage gaps (docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md,
# tgs-digest). Real, notable episode: Nate and Jancovici had never
# encountered each other's work before this recording, yet arrived at
# nearly identical conclusions independently — genuine convergent
# evolution of the same worldview from two entirely separate paths.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_84_JeanMarcJancovici a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jean-Marc Jancovici: \"Our Global Energy Predicament\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with French energy/climate consultant Jean-Marc Jancovici (Carbone 4, The Shift Project), opened by Nate's own direct framing of its significance: neither had encountered the other's work before this recording, and yet 'we're saying almost the same things,' which he calls 'a robust finding.' Jancovici delivers what Nate calls, unprompted, a better summary of the human predicament than his own — fossil fuels replacing hard human labor with machine labor over two centuries, population growing from 1 to 8 billion, life expectancy roughly doubling, and civilization's material and monetary systems built on non-renewable resources that don't register as scarce because they carry no price. Traces his own intellectual path through three real 'red pill' moments: discovering the scale of the climate challenge while doing telecom business plans in the late 1990s, recognizing energy as the actual driver of national development patterns, and finally recognizing that democracies structurally cannot react quickly to slow-moving, diffuse threats."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-07-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-08-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JeanMarcJancovici ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/84-jean-marc-jancovici> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-EHCguJp9eQ> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/TGS84Jean-MarcJancoviciTranscript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter, tgs:Work.LePlanDeTransformation, tgs:Work.LeMondeSansFin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The episode's real, most distinctive conceptual contribution is Jancovici's own direct three-way distinction: efficiency (same service, less energy), 'sobriété' (deliberately forgoing a good or service to conserve resources — biking instead of driving, by choice), and poverty (the identical physical outcome, but not chosen). His own real, pointed claim is that what French politicians now call 'sobriété' in the wake of the real 2022-23 European energy-price shock is, in his direct assessment, actually poverty being relabeled — 'no government can use the word poverty,' so 'sobriété' gives the same forced reduction a more palatable, agency-implying name. He's candid and specific that he doesn't believe democracies will voluntarily choose degrowth ahead of a real shock forcing the issue — 'you have the strategy of your assets,' a real, recurring phrase he uses to argue nations act according to their real historical constraints and resources, not their stated wishes. This framing extends into a real, pointed cross-national comparison: he argues resource-constrained nations with real histories of hardship (France, Japan, China) are culturally better positioned to accept limits than resource-abundant 'young' nations built on the appearance of limitlessness (the US, Brazil, Canada, Russia) — delivered with a direct, self-aware caveat to Nate that he's 'being a little bit rude' to an American audience. Real, concrete numbers ground his own public reach: TGS_84 itself is a real convergence point for a public career built substantially on independent digital reach (roughly a million French-language video views, 500,000+ copies of his graphic novel, 100,000+ of his policy essay) rather than traditional media gatekeeping. His own real magic-wand answer is uncharacteristically blunt for an engineer: 'suppress greed' — followed by a direct, unhedged claim that the deepest debate isn't really about which energy technology to build, but whether humans can put ethics above greed at all: 'it's much easier to build a nuclear reactor than changing our minds.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Christophe Blain (illustrator, tgs:Work.LeMondeSansFin's real co-creator, not yet a Persona); Reiner Kümmel (real energy economist, directly cited alongside the Solow Residual) — both real, deferred. Real, direct confirmation of a shared 'big four/five horsemen' framework already present elsewhere in this graph in various forms (financial system, geopolitics, complexity, the social contract — Nate's own words here) — no dedicated Concept individual exists yet for this recurring framework; kept as prose, consistent with how this graph has handled the same gap in other recent builds (Frankly_88, Frankly_126)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-175 (Jean-Marc Jancovici, real second
# appearance), from a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by
# MJSullivan. Real, deliberate check against the anti-repetition rule
# (wwns-methodology.md §6, tgs-digest): the sobriété/poverty distinction
# is restated briefly as an opening callback (real, direct overlap with
# TGS_84), but the substantive bulk of this episode is genuinely new
# ground — geopolitics, AI, and a real, distinct theoretical framework
# for why humans turn to fossil fuels in the first place. A future WWNS
# piece citing sobriété specifically should use TGS_84 (the original,
# fuller discussion); this episode's own real, different angles are
# fair game independently, per that same rule's explicit exception.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_175_JeanMarcJancovici a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Jean-Marc Jancovici: \"Sobriete vs Poverty: Preparing for a New Cultural Paradigm\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Jancovici's real second TGS appearance, prompted by the American release of his and Christophe Blain's graphic novel 'World Without End' (over a million copies sold in France). Opens with a brief real restatement of the efficiency/sobriety/poverty distinction from TGS_84 before covering substantially new ground: real details of adapting the book for American readers (French examples replaced with American ones, unit conversions, and — for real copyright reasons — swapping the original French version's Iron Man analogy for an original character, 'Armor Man,' since Marvel would have required licensing fees), the real divergence between US and European responses to energy/geopolitical pressure, and a real, distinct theoretical account of why humans turn to fossil fuels in the first place: innate laziness (least-effort optimization, shared with other animals), an accumulation instinct Jancovici ties to mid-latitude civilizations needing to store resources through winter, and status-seeking rooted in ancestral group-living dynamics."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-04-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JeanMarcJancovici ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/jean-marc-jancovici-sobriete-vs-poverty-preparing-for-a-new-cultural-paradigm> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=59WB0im3lLg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TGS175Jean-MarcJancoviciTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_84_JeanMarcJancovici, tgs:Work.LeMondeSansFin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, concrete illustration of intellectual-property constraints opens the substantive conversation: the French edition's central metaphor — humanity's fleet of energy-powered machines as equivalent to wearing an Iron Man suit — couldn't be used in the American edition without Marvel's licensing, so Jancovici and Blain invented an original character, 'Armor Man,' to carry the same idea. Real, specific adaptation choices follow: swapping French leisure examples for American ones (a couple's shift from backyard hiking to Colorado ski trips), and recalculating the book's own real hypothetical water-reservoir-for-two-weeks-of-electricity-storage figure for US rather than French/European scale. The geopolitical section is grounded in real, current 2025 events — Trump's re-election, US-brokered Ukraine peace negotiations, US support for Israel — with Jancovici direct about a real cultural asymmetry: US energy self-sufficiency versus total European fossil fuel dependence means the same sobriety argument lands as pure values-based idealism in America but as literal practical necessity in Europe, where he says 'the two merge perfectly.' A real, concrete labor-transition question threads through the systemic-change discussion: using French trucking as an example (600,000 jobs, a hypothetical decade-long reduction to 400,000), Jancovici argues explicitly that failing to answer 'what do you do with the people' is a direct, real driver of populist party support — offering a genuine plan for worker transition as the actual antidote to that political backlash, not just an economic detail. The episode's real theoretical center is Jancovici's own three-part account of why humans turn to fossil fuels at all: innate laziness (shared with other animals, now expressed as modern comfort-seeking), an accumulation instinct he ties specifically to mid-latitude civilizations needing to survive winters (contrasted directly with less accumulative equatorial African societies), and status-seeking rooted in ancestral group-living, where status once meant direct access to food and mates. His own proposed inversion is notably concrete rather than abstract: historically, monastic restraint has itself carried high status in some periods, offered as real, existing proof that low-consumption living can be re-status-ified rather than requiring humans to fight their own psychology."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Peter Strack (TGS #166, '2000 Watt Society') and Alexis Zeigler (TGS #171, Living Energy Farm — a real intentional community reportedly living on 3% of average American per-capita energy use), both directly cited by Nate as real, recent guests; Frankly #87 ('Some of the Central Questions of Our Time'), directly cross-referenced; a real Pew citation on the 32% of US adults expressing support for authoritarian government — worth checking against the existing WWNS candidate list for possible overlap or complementary use."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-75 (Kris De Decker, real second appearance),
# from a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
# Closes one of the real repeat-guest coverage gaps
# (docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, tgs-digest). His real first appearance
# (TGS_33, "Low Tech: What, Why and How") is directly confirmed via this
# episode's own show notes but not yet built in this graph.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_75_KrisDeDecker a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Kris De Decker: \"Looking Back Towards a Human Powered Future\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with low-tech researcher Kris De Decker (Low-Tech Magazine, Barcelona), a deliberate deeper dive into five categories of appropriate technology — real, concrete alternatives to high-tech defaults for heating, cooling, transportation, and more. Opens with Nate's own real framing for why this matters: the show's usual concepts land cognitively but not emotionally, since 'we're not forced to do it yet' — directly citing a real prior conversation with someone in Lebanon (a 50% GDP collapse) already living the reduced-consumption future most listeners can only imagine. De Decker's central, vivid reframing treats human muscle power as a real, legitimate energy source in its own right — comparable to roughly one square meter of solar panel on a sunny day, but unlike solar, available on demand: 'humans are base load,' in Nate's own memorable phrase."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-05-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.KrisDeDecker ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/75-kris-de-decker> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4MYqRvm7vX4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/TGS75KrisDeDeckerTranscript.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct personal demonstration grounds the abstract argument: De Decker runs a real hybrid solar/human-power system in his own Barcelona apartment, using a bike generator (built around a wind-turbine-style charge controller) to shrink the battery storage his system needs — battery costs and embodied energy typically account for 70-80% of an off-grid system's total burden. He's candid that combining human and renewable power isn't really about the electricity itself, but about the psychological shift it forces: producing your own power makes you 'think twice about how much power you need' in a way that supply-side renewable-energy discourse alone never does. The conversation's real emotional throughline is De Decker's own repeated argument that most needed change is social, not technical — 'communal household tasks' (shared kitchens, laundries, bathhouses, modeled on real historical precedents from Roman bathhouses to Soviet communal apartments) are framed explicitly as 'social innovation instead of technological innovation,' requiring no new components, just a different mindset about what's normal to do alone versus together. A real, extended detour into US healthcare closes the conversation on a genuinely provocative note: De Decker argues a substantial share of modern medical resource use (single-use plastics, energy-intensive sterilization, aggressive treatment of otherwise self-limiting conditions) reflects profit incentives and defensive practice rather than health necessity, citing a real case study suggesting some historical, less-resource-intensive medical practices produced comparable outcomes. The episode closes on real, unscripted warmth — Nate half-jokingly proposes he'd move into a De Decker-branded communal apartment building 'as long as they allowed dogs' — before De Decker previews a real, specific future research direction: an in-progress piece on the history of steel production and the real difficulty of 'escaping the Iron Age' given how much supposedly sustainable technology still depends on it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Joslin Faith Kehdy (real TGS episode #73, the Lebanon-based sustainable-packaging guest Nate references directly and calls 'my friend' — not yet built); Robert Lustig and John Kitzhaber (both real, directly cited TGS guests with real episode links, neither yet built). DJ White and Simon Michaux are both directly cited by name, but only their real panel appearances (RR-11/RR-24 for White; RR-1 for Michaux) are currently built in this graph — their own real solo episodes (TGS #51 for White; #19 and #49 for Michaux) remain unbuilt, so dct:references was deliberately not asserted here rather than pointing at individuals that don't yet exist."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-30 — TGS-194 (Luke Kemp, real second appearance), from
# a complete real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
# Closes one of the real repeat-guest coverage gaps
# (docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, tgs-digest). His real first appearance
# (TGS_153, "Existential Risks") is directly confirmed via this
# episode's own transcript and show notes but not yet built.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_194_LukeKemp a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Past and Future of Societal Collapse: Why Civilizations Fall and What We Can Learn From It"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with existential-risk researcher Luke Kemp, timed to the release of his new book, Goliath's Curse. Kemp opens by directly dismantling the term 'civilization' itself — historically vague, checklist-dependent (writing, cities, agriculture — none universally present even in societies conventionally called civilizations, like the writing-free Incan Empire), and loaded with colonial-era propaganda connotations traced to a Latin root implying virtue and restraint that early hierarchical societies (marked by mass human sacrifice, warfare, patriarchy) rarely displayed. Proposes 'Goliath' instead: a society organized around dominance hierarchies enforced through violence, distinguishing it directly from real, documented counterexamples of large, complex, non-hierarchical settlements — the Indus Valley/Harappan civilization and early Teotihuacán, both cited with specific real archaeological evidence. Nate's own opening frames the real, direct parallel between Kemp's Goliath framework and this show's own Superorganism concept explicitly."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LukeKemp ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/194-luke-kemp> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W7JsDrHrRsI> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/TGS-194-Luke-Kemp-Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.GoliathsCurse, tgs:Work.TheMythOfTheMachine ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, striking exosomatic-energy comparison anchors the conversation's material argument: the average hunter-gatherer captures roughly 4,000 calories per day, against roughly 230,000 for the average modern European — a genuinely vast real gap Kemp attributes substantially to conspicuous consumption and status competition rather than baseline need, directly citing real data that the wealthiest 1% of the world's wealth-holders account for more emissions than the poorest 66%. Kemp's own real, distinctive contribution to the conversation's forward-looking section is 'Silicon Goliath' — his own term for AI-enabled mass surveillance states, which he argues, with real, direct urgency, may be closer to society's current default trajectory than either collapse or genuine democratic reform, and which will be the real subject of his next book. The episode's most candid, unscripted moment comes when Nate directly asks whether Kemp's worldview has shifted since their first conversation roughly six months earlier: Kemp discloses, unprompted and pre-empting real anticipated criticism ('I'm sure there'll be comments about hopium'), that he has become substantially more hopeful about people and the future — a real, genuine change from what he describes as an initially grim view of humans without external authority, credited directly to engaging with open-democracy literature and real, small-scale initiatives already underway. Nate's own closing question is left genuinely, deliberately unresolved rather than answered: whether growing public awareness of Goliath-style dominance dynamics will accelerate a society's unwinding or help prevent it. The conversation closes with a real, stated intention from Nate to have Kemp on a future Reality Roundtable — not yet realized as a built episode."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Audrey Tang's real TGS episode is now confirmed with an exact number (#169) via this episode's own show notes — previously known in this graph only as a real, repeatedly-deferred citation without a confirmed episode number; Hélène Landemore (Open Democracy, directly cited alongside Tang), Lewis Mumford (The Myth of the Machine, directly cited), and Dario Amodei's own real, directly-quoted Axios interview ('a 25% chance that things go really, really badly') are all real, deferred citations. Nate's own real academic paper, 'Economics for the future — Beyond the superorganism' (ScienceDirect), is directly cited in this episode's own show notes by its real, specific title — not yet independently modeled as its own Work individual in this graph, consistent with this graph's established practice of not over-minting Nate's own writing without clear need."@en ;
    .

