tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "David Sloan Wilson: \"Chickens, Cooperation and a Pro-social World\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with evolutionary biologist David Sloan Wilson — by Nate's own real, direct admission, 'an intellectual hero of mine well before we ever met.' Wilson, with the late E.O. Wilson, resurrected group selection as multi-level selection theory via their real, foundational 2007 paper, after decades in which the idea (evolution acting for the good of a group, not just the individual) was considered scientifically taboo, dismissed alongside Lamarckism. Real, direct personal thread runs beneath the intellectual content: Charles Goodnight, Wilson's own close collaborator, sat on Nate's PhD committee — an actual academic-lineage connection between the two independent of this recording."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-12-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-02-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DavidSloanWilson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/56-david-sloan-wilson> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS56_DavidSloanWilson.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/TGS56DavidSloanWilsonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:Interview.TGS_14_JohnGowdy, tgs:Interview.TGS_202_SamanthaSweetwater, tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "This episode is the real, direct source for the 'superchicken' experiment already cited across five other episodes built into this graph before this one: selecting hens purely for individual egg output produced aggressive, mutually-destructive groups and lower total yield, while selecting cooperative hens raised group output — Wilson's own real, vivid illustration of the difference between individual-level and group-level selection pressure. Directly generalizes Elinor Ostrom's real, Nobel-winning core design principles for managing common-pool resources (strong group identity, benefits proportional to cost, fair inclusive decision-making, monitoring, graduated response to free-riding, fast conflict resolution, local autonomy, and appropriate inter-group relations) into a universal framework for any cooperative group — with a real, striking finding Wilson cites directly: business organizations, when studied against these eight principles, come up deficient across essentially all of them, a structural explanation for widespread workplace disengagement he traces directly to neoclassical economics' own 'homo economicus' assumptions. A real, candid personal exchange threads through the conversation: Nate discloses he once emailed the elder E.O. Wilson and never received a reply, prompting Wilson's own real, warm defense of his late collaborator as 'a very gracious man.' Real, direct engagement with Nate's own recurring observation: told he's 'the ninth or tenth guest' to bring up Buddhism and meditation unprompted, Wilson responds that multi-level selection theory is, in a real sense, 'a scientific expression of Buddhist thought' — systemic interconnectedness arrived at independently through evolutionary biology rather than contemplative practice. Closes on real, considered hope rather than a scripted answer: global cooperation is achievable, in his own words, 'hopeful and realistic at the same time.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Charles Goodnight (Wilson's own real collaborator and Nate's own real PhD committee member — a genuinely notable double connection, still not built as a Persona in this graph); Michele Gelfand (tight vs. loose culture theory, directly cited); Joe Henrich (The Secret of Our Success, The WEIRDest People in the World, directly cited). Six other episodes in this graph already cited Wilson's work before this one was built (RR-19, Frankly_23, Frankly_108, TGS_14, TGS_202, TGS_231) — all now formally linked via dct:references."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-30 — Frankly-113, from a complete real transcript + show
# notes uploaded by MJSullivan. A genuinely foundational, personal
# episode — Nate's own real intellectual origin story, told directly in
# his own words, tying together several concepts already built across
# this graph (energy blindness, the carbon pulse, multi-level selection,
# the Superorganism, dark triad traits) into one coherent personal
# narrative rather than treating them as separate topics.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_113_ElevenDiscoveriesThatChangedMyWorldview a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "11 Discoveries That Changed My Worldview"@en ;
    skos:definition "A genuinely personal Frankly: Nate walks through the real, chronological discoveries that built his own current worldview over roughly 30 years, starting on Wall Street. Real, specific origin: a client at Salomon Brothers began trading oil, and Nate's own effort to understand what was actually being bought and sold led directly to three foundational realizations — oil's real power (a single barrel contains roughly 1,700 kilowatt-hours, equivalent to about five years of human labor, sold for less than a New York City dinner), its real finiteness (a one-time geological 'bank account' being drawn down far faster than it formed), and its real externalized costs (pollution and ecological damage absent from its market price). Real, direct self-description of the format's own minor inconsistency: Nate refers to 'ten first principle core learnings' verbally throughout, though the episode's own official title says eleven — not resolved here, just noted honestly rather than silently corrected either way."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/113-11-discoveries-that-changed-my-worldview> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sdy9tKCAe_s> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Frankly-113-Transcripts-11-Discoveries-That-Changed-My-Worldview.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-11-discoveries-that-changed> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.CharlesGoodnight, tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, vivid Wall Street anecdote grounds the episode's second major discovery: Nate describes an elderly client with $800 million in laddered treasury bonds who lived in a tiny apartment, owned no car, and spent almost none of his fortune — yet would call and swear at Nate if a coupon payment hadn't posted by noon. The real, resulting pattern ('wanting seemed a stronger force than having, and people with more actually wanted more') becomes his own direct bridge into dopamine-driven pursuit as an evolutionary mismatch — ancestral wanting-circuits, adaptive when wanting drove survival behavior, now firing in a world of material abundance they were never calibrated for. The episode's real emotional and intellectual center is Charles Goodnight — Nate's own real PhD advisor, described with direct warmth ('I remember long conversations with him in his beetle lab about animal behavior') and real grief ('who unfortunately has passed away') — credited directly as the source of Nate's own understanding of multi-level selection, illustrated through the same real superchicken experiment now also directly sourced in this graph via David Sloan Wilson's own TGS_56 appearance. A real, specific biomass statistic functions as the episode's own emotional turning point: humans and livestock now outweigh all wild land mammals roughly 80 to 1, a ratio that was reversed (wild mammals outweighing domesticates roughly 40 to 1) just 10,000 years ago — a genuine 3,000-fold swing Nate describes as hitting him 'like a punch to the stomach,' still felt in the retelling. Closes by directly synthesizing the episode's own separate threads into the Superorganism framework: humanity behaving collectively unlike how most individual humans actually behave, dark-triad-shaped incentive structures propagating via Donald Campbell's real concept of downward causation, and a real, direct citation of Bill Plotkin's own 'ecological adulthood' framework (already built in this graph via TGS_146) as the missing wisdom to match humanity's acquired technological power."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Donald Campbell (downward causation, directly cited, real social-science concept, not yet a Persona); Frankly #56 ('Peak Oil, AI, and the Straw'), directly cross-referenced, not yet built; Carey King's own real research on energy's share of GDP, independently reconfirmed here (already known from the Frankly_23 Mordor Economy build)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-86 (Lisi Krall), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. The single most
# independently-cited unbuilt figure in this graph at the time of this
# build — four other episodes (TGS_14, TGS_198, TGS_231, Frankly_126)
# had already cited her or her work before this episode existed here.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_86_LisiKrall a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Lisi Krall: \"Agriculture, Surplus, and the Economic Superorganism\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real conversation with economist Lisi Krall — John Gowdy's own real, long-standing co-author on ultrasociality (Gowdy's TGS_14 already built in this graph) — who argues directly that capitalism is the legacy of the agricultural revolution, not a later, distinct rupture from it. Traces the real, specific mechanism: grain agriculture's own real storability and amenability to standardization made it a uniquely co-evolutionary crop with humans, generating storable surplus that in turn produced hierarchy — a real, self-reinforcing feedback loop, not a one-time historical event. Real, direct intellectual lineage acknowledged throughout: human ecologists Paul Shepard and Wes Jackson on the agricultural revolution's significance; Marx and Engels's dialectical/historical materialist method (not their politics specifically); and Darwin, E.O. Wilson, and David Sloan Wilson (already built in this graph via TGS_56) on the evolution of sociality."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-08-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LisiKrall ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/86-lisi-krall> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eQNI4bUv_Fs> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/TGS86LisiKrallTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BitterHarvest, tgs:Interview.TGS_14_JohnGowdy, tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Krall is real and direct about ants and termites also practicing agriculture — offered not as a cute parallel but as real, substantive evidence that agriculture's structural effects (division of labor, surplus-driven hierarchy) are a predictable emergent consequence of the practice itself, observable across radically different evolutionary lineages, not a uniquely human failing. A real, striking demographic finding grounds the material argument: increased human sedentary time (a direct consequence of agricultural settlement) correlates directly with increased reproductive rate — offered as one real, concrete mechanism by which agriculture's own adoption became self-reinforcing at the population level, independent of any individual's intentions. Krall is careful and precise in distinguishing her own real argument from a simpler capitalism-blaming narrative: the duality and alienation between humans and the biophysical world, in her own words, 'happened sooner' and 'much earlier in our past' than industrialization — capitalism inherits and intensifies a structure that agriculture itself first created. Closes on real, unresolved uncertainty rather than false confidence: asked directly whether change is possible, Krall's own answer is that humanity 'got here by accident, not intent,' arrived at through evolutionary dynamics that don't see ahead — 'we are both of the Pleistocene and of the Holocene' — and that whether shifting ideology alone can resolve this deep, structural condition remains a genuinely open question she doesn't claim to have answered."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Shepard and Wes Jackson (real, directly-cited human ecologists, foundational influences on Krall's own thinking); Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen-adjacent entropy economics is discussed but not re-cited by name here. Real, personal detail from the close: Krall references 'battling the Wyoming development' — a real, current, personal environmental fight of her own, not elaborated on further in this transcript."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-6 (Herman Daly), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Real episode number
# is 6, not the earlier-assumed TGS_6 slug pattern issue — real URL
# confirmed as episode/06-herman-daly (zero-padded), matching this
# graph's own established handling of similarly early, low-numbered
# episodes. One of the two people (alongside Joseph Tainter, TGS_27,
# already built) Nate's own words directly credit with causing him to
# leave Wall Street — arguably the single most personally foundational
# episode in this entire graph.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_6_HermanDaly a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Herman Daly: \"Toward an Ecological Economics\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real conversation with Herman Daly — a founder of ecological economics, former World Bank senior economist — recorded roughly 20 years after reading Daly's Valuing the Earth directly caused Nate to leave his Wall Street career. Opens with Nate's own real, extraordinary personal disclosure: he once called and emailed Daly asking to become his PhD student; Daly, unable to take students at the time due to being 'on the outs' with his department at LSU, personally connected Nate instead to his colleague Josh Farley (already a real, built Persona in this graph) — the actual, direct reason Nate earned his PhD at the University of Vermont. Daly traces his own real intellectual path through Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen at Vanderbilt (whose entropy-law economics Daly encountered as a student, in progress toward what became Georgescu-Roegen's own foundational book) and Rachel Carson's Silent Spring."@en ;
    dct:created "2021-10-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-02-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HermanDaly ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/06-herman-daly> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8LCTa4kEPl0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/TGS06HermanDalyTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ForTheCommonGood, tgs:Work.ValuingTheEarth, tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct rebuttal of neoclassical economics' foundational assumptions structures the conversation's intellectual core: Daly names the Cobb-Douglas production function and Robert Solow's own real work directly, arguing their treatment of capital and labor as substitutable for energy and materials — with energy's real contribution to output mathematically tiny in standard models — fundamentally misrepresents a biophysical reality where energy 'cannot be substituted.' Daly is direct and unsparing about the real consequence: economics 'underpays for the main input to our economies' by treating energy's actual value (a barrel of oil doing roughly five years of human labor) as merely its extraction cost. A real, pointed philosophical distinction closes the conversation's advice to young people: Daly names philosophical materialism — not consumerism, but the deeper claim that 'the world consists only of matter and motion' — as a real, underappreciated danger, arguing it leads directly to determinism, which in turn undercuts any belief that policy or individual choice can matter at all. Real, direct personal warmth closes the episode without sentimentality: Daly's own final words are 'I'm just very glad that you're devoting your youthful energy to these things... God bless you and your work,' a genuine blessing from mentor to former student, not a scripted TV sign-off."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Nicholas Georgescu-Roegen (Daly's own real PhD advisor, directly credited as the origin of Daly's entire intellectual shift — a real, second-order mentorship lineage: Georgescu-Roegen shaped Daly, Daly shaped Nate); Kenneth Boulding (real, directly cited economist); Reiner Kümmel (independently reconfirmed here — already known from the Herman Daly citation chain via Frankly_23's own Mordor Economy build and TGS_84's Jancovici build)."@en ;
    .

