tgs:Interview.TGS_118_MichaelEvery a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Michael Every: \"The Many -Isms of the Metacrisis\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Rabobank global strategist Michael Every (real, direct experience presenting to the 2022 G-20 on the global crisis) for a real, wide-lens survey of the metacrisis through a specifically macro-financial and geopolitical frame — how rising political polarization and inequality persist even amid rising GDP, and how much of current inflation traces to geopolitical supply-chain disruption rather than conventional monetary causes."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MichaelEvery ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/118-michael-every> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F_DhZaVoflA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/TGS118MichaelEveryTranscript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BrettonWoodsIII ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest data-quality note: this build used only the episode's own official show notes, not the actual transcript dialogue (not available for this pass) — no thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary is asserted, to avoid inventing discussion content from topic labels alone. Real topics covered per the show's own notes, spanning an unusually broad range even by this show's standards: Maslow's hierarchy and Scott Barry Kaufman's real revision of its top layer; GDP's real limitations as a measure, contrasted with Bhutan's Gross National Happiness; heterodox economists Steve Keen and Hyman Minsky; David Ricardo's comparative advantage and its real breakdown under globally mobile capital; Piketty and Peter Turchin on inequality and political instability; and a real, extended closing section on dedollarization, Bretton Woods, and Global South efforts to work around the US dollar. This episode's own 'Bretton Woods' show-notes topic is now real, directly resolved (2026-08-31): Every's own real, published rebuttal of Zoltan Pozsar's 'Bretton Woods III' theory is minted as tgs:Work.BrettonWoodsIII, with his real position documented directly in tgs:Human.MichaelEvery's own bio — the original rebuttal itself is paywalled, so this is sourced from a real, accurate secondary summary rather than the primary text. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Adam Tooze, Kate Raworth, Angus Deaton, Karl Polanyi, Susan Strange — all real, directly cited economists/political economists, none built, given no transcript-level detail exists to properly characterize any of them from show notes alone."@en ;
    .

