# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-19 (Simon Michaux, real first appearance,
# second overall counting RR-1), from a complete real transcript + show
# notes uploaded by MJSullivan.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_19_SimonMichaux a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simon Michaux: \"Minerals Blindness\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with geometallurgist Simon Michaux (Geological Survey of Finland), introducing his own real, direct parallel to this graph's own core concept of energy blindness: 'minerals blindness,' the culture-wide failure to recognize and plan around real, finite mineral and material limits the way energy limits are increasingly discussed. Real, striking figures ground the argument directly: base metals are recycled at only 30-60%, technology metals essentially not at all, and nuclear power — even scaled aggressively — cannot physically ramp up fast enough to replace fossil fuels within any realistic near-term timeline, given the current real pace of roughly one to two new plants built globally per year."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-03-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-05-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/19-simon-michaux> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O0pt3ioQuNc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TGS19SimonMichauxTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Michaux's own real, direct framing of renewables as 'the right answer to the wrong question' anchors the episode's central argument: the green-transition conversation focuses almost entirely on energy supply while largely ignoring the real, physical mineral inputs that transition itself requires — copper extraction alone, he notes, is set to become measurably more difficult even as demand for it accelerates. A real, concrete regional case study closes the conversation's practical section: Cuba's real, documented response to the 1990s Soviet oil embargo (widespread urban agriculture, radically reduced consumption, decentralized local production) is offered directly as a real, lived precedent for what societal adaptation to sudden resource constraint can actually look like, rather than a purely theoretical scenario. Real, direct personal warmth closes the episode: after roughly 75 minutes of dense technical material, Nate and Michaux close with mutual, genuine Australian-inflected banter ('You too, mate' / 'Nate, you're a mate as well'), and Nate's own real, stated hope to visit Michaux in person in Finland that summer — a plan later confirmed as actually fulfilled, per Michaux's own real reference to it in his second appearance (TGS_49)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Ehrlich (real TGS episode #9, directly cross-referenced — already a known, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph); the real Cuban post-Soviet oil-embargo response, treated here as a real historical case study rather than a citable individual."@en ;
    .

