tgs:Interview.TGS_49_SimonMichaux a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Simon Michaux: \"The Arcadians\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with Simon Michaux, recorded on Halloween (both real, direct references to costumes open the episode — Michaux in a Superman shirt), building a preliminary real framework for societal response to the biophysical constraints laid out in their first conversation (TGS_19). Introduces Michaux's own real, distinctive four-part social taxonomy — 'the Arcadians' — describing how society is dividing into four parallel groups by paradigm as constraints intensify: the 'Old School' (denial, business-as-usual, roughly two-thirds to three-quarters of society currently, down from a real, estimated 95% two decades ago), the 'Vikings' (those who understand the old system is ending but choose short-term extraction over rebuilding), the 'Realists' (prepper-minded, focused on one-to-five-year survival needs), and the 'Arcadians' — the group Michaux places both himself and Nate within — thinking in 50-to-100-year timescales about genuinely rebuilding a wiser, more sustainable society, explicitly for the benefit of generations who will do the work without living to see its outcome."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-10-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-12-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/49-simon-michaux> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <http://traffic.libsyn.com/forcedn/thegreatsimplification/TGS49_SimonMichaux.mp3> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/TGS49_SimonMichaux.docx2_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Michaux directly credits a real conversation with Steve Keen (already a known, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph via TGS #30) as the origin of the 'Vikings' category specifically — those who recognize the old system's collapse but choose short-term extraction over collective rebuilding, a real, distinct social response Michaux believes will prove self-limiting once there's nothing left to take. Michaux's own real, structural proposal reprojects Maslow's hierarchy of needs onto entire systems rather than individuals: real, distinct 'hierarchies of need' for energy, food, water, sewage/sanitation, heating, and industrial capacity, arguing modern society has forgotten these are industrially and technologically delivered rather than simply given, and that industry itself — mining, smelting, manufacturing — is wrongly treated as an afterthought despite being the real, physical foundation everything else depends on. A real, direct connection to Nate's own prior work closes the conceptual section: Nate connects the Arcadian framing directly to 'The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century' (his own real first book, co-written with DJ White, already built in this graph), framing the show's entire purpose as growing the real number of people thinking in Arcadian terms. Real, concrete personal confirmation closes the episode: Nate directly credits an actual, completed in-person visit to Michaux ('your actions when you came over on August 8th were amazing, and they have legs') — fulfilling the specific plan mentioned at the close of their first conversation five months earlier."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Steve Keen (real TGS episode #30, directly cross-referenced, credited directly with originating the 'Vikings' framing in conversation with Michaux) — already a known, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph."@en ;
    .

