# ADDED 2026-08-31 — Frankly-98, from a complete real transcript + show
# notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Real, direct sequel to Frankly_97
# (already built) — a real, distinctive framework in its own right,
# and the source of a third independent citation of Prigogine's
# 'islands of coherence,' now minted as its own Concept.
#############################################

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_98_TenQualitiesThatCouldChangeTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "10 Qualities That Could Change the Future: The Seeds of New Cultural Mitochondria"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct sequel to the prior week's Frankly (Frankly_97, already built), extending its closing reference to 'cultural mitochondria' into a full, structured framework: 10 real, named internal human traits Nate proposes as the distributed, largely invisible 'metabolic engines' of whatever cultural system eventually succeeds the economic Superorganism. Frames this directly against Marvin Harris's own real cultural materialism (infrastructure, structure, superstructure) — arguing Harris's own framework subsumed the inner lives of individual humans into 'beliefs and values' without examining them directly, a gap Nate proposes to address."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-06-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-06-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/98-10-qualities-that-could-change-the-future> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zrGsRAv2MrY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Frankly-98-Transcript-10-Qualities-That-Could-Change-the-Future.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-10-qualities-that-could-change> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller, tgs:Persona.MarvinHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct personal candor threads through several of the ten traits rather than presenting them as abstract ideals: describing 'groundedness,' Nate directly acknowledges his own real, gradual change since the podcast began — 'more grounded than I used to be,' crediting sustained personal work rather than claiming arrival. The 'post-tragic' trait is credited directly to either Marc Gafni or Zak Stein (Nate genuinely unsure which, both already built in this graph) — distinguishing pre-tragic (unaware), tragic (aware but not yet metabolized), and post-tragic (grief integrated, still capable of engaged action) as real, distinct stages, with a direct cross-reference to Francis Weller (already built via TGS_167) on grief work specifically. A real, personal disclosure of felt rather than merely intellectual change closes the interconnectedness trait: Nate describes a real shift from loving nature 'from me' to actually feeling himself 'meshing in, merging in with the ecosystem' during recent time at his own real 'sit spot' — distinguishing knowing interconnectedness abstractly from actually feeling it. The tenth and final trait, 'play and joy,' is offered with real, direct personal humility as optional but genuine: 'something I've learned personally, so I'm throwing it in here' — humor and levity framed directly as a real survival trait and source of resilience, not mere distraction from gravity. Closes, as in Frankly_65, with Prigogine's own real 'islands of coherence' concept (now independently cited across at least three episodes in this graph, minted here as tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence), extended directly to the episode's own central metaphor: 'culture doesn't change by decree. It changes by mitochondria. Us.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Marc Gafni (real, directly cited alongside Zak Stein as the possible originator of 'post-tragic,' credited with genuine uncertainty by Nate himself, not built)."@en ;
    .

