tgs:Interview.TGS_197_OveHoeghGuldberg a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Will Coral Reefs Be Gone by 2050? How Bleaching, Acidification, and Ocean Heating are Killing Coral Reefs"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with marine biologist Ove Hoegh-Guldberg, timed to the 25th anniversary — halfway point — of his own real, landmark 1999 paper predicting global coral reef loss by 2050. Hoegh-Guldberg opens with real, direct candor about that original prediction's reception: 'many scientists wouldn't really support the conclusions at first because it just seemed incredible,' and his own real, memorable framing of the stakes — if he turned out wrong, 'that would be the sweetest egg I'd ever had.' A real, striking acceleration anchors the update: bleaching events once occurred every five to seven years, allowing recovery time; the past decade has seen 'back to back bleaching' with no recovery window at all, corals now dying faster than they can be replaced."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.OveHoeghGuldberg ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/197-ove-hoegh-guldberg> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8DKxpUFYmnA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/TGS-197-Ove-Hoegh-Guldberg-Transcript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, striking figures anchor the ecological stakes directly: roughly 25% of all oceanic biodiversity lives in or around coral reefs, and over 500 million people depend directly on reef ecosystems for food security and livelihoods. Hoegh-Guldberg is real and direct that his own research focus has shifted over the decades from pure science toward communication and policy engagement, spending substantial time with politicians directly — describing a real, current moment of political alignment in Australia across Labor, a reforming Liberal party, and the Greens as a genuine, rare opportunity worth acting on quickly. A real, unscripted moment of political candor surfaces near the close: Hoegh-Guldberg remarks that 'the Americans don't' share that same alignment, prompting Nate's own real, immediate apology — which Hoegh-Guldberg directly waves off, careful to distinguish the country from its current leadership, 'you're now being tainted by a brush which not everyone owns.' Real, concrete grounds for hope close the substantive conversation: Hoegh-Guldberg estimates that 25-30% of the emissions gap between a 3°C and 1.5°C future could be closed using off-the-shelf technology already available today, including real, direct mention of floating offshore wind. Closes on real, considered resolve rather than despair: 'keep the faith and get active and let's solve this problem together.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Johan Rockström (real, directly cited multiple times, his own real TGS episode #134 confirmed but not built); Anastassia Makarieva (real TGS episode #193, biotic pump, independently reconfirmed here — already known as a deferred citation elsewhere in this graph, e.g. Craig Tindale's TGS_207); Carlos Nobre (real TGS episode #150, independently reconfirmed here — also already known as deferred, e.g. via Andrew Millison's TGS_178); Stephen Jenkinson (real TGS episode #190, directly cross-referenced alongside Francis Weller — already built — and RR-17 — also already built — as this graph's real grief-focused episodes)."@en ;
    .

