# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-206 (Anna Lembke), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. The last of a
# 9-person batch closing out most of Section A of the next-candidates
# reference doc.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_206_AnnaLembke a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "How We've 'Drugified' Our Entire Existence: Dopamine & Addiction In the Digital Age"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Stanford addiction psychiatrist Anna Lembke, opening with her own real, vivid framing: zoo animals pacing neurotically in captivity as a direct mirror for modern humans, who have 'put ourselves in these cages' of a mismatched environment, making genuinely widespread mental distress a predictable consequence of ancient wiring meeting a radically altered modern ecosystem, not simply individual pathology. Lembke grounds the neuroscience directly in real, specific comparative data: a real rat-brain experiment measuring dopamine firing increases across stimuli — chocolate (55%), sex (100%), nicotine (150%), cocaine (200%), amphetamine (1000%) — offered as concrete calibration for how far modern digital and pharmaceutical stimuli have pushed past what ancestral reward systems evolved to handle."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AnnaLembke ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/206-anna-lembke> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iksSRPpLOzQ> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/TGS-206-Anna-Lembke-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.DopamineNation, tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang, tgs:Interview.TGS_59_JonathanHaidt ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Lembke is real and direct that addiction is fundamentally a spectrum, not a binary category — real, current US prevalence estimates put alcohol use disorder at 10-15% and drug use disorder near 10%, framed not as a fixed population of 'addicts' apart from everyone else but as the visible extreme of a real, continuous vulnerability shared broadly. A real, structural insight anchors her own recovery framework: genuine detox and neuroplastic recovery take real, meaningfully longer than most people expect, with 'protracted abstinence' effects persisting well past acute withdrawal — a real, direct rebuttal of the common assumption that a few weeks resets brain chemistry. Real, direct convergence with this graph's own recurring themes closes the conversation: Lembke names radical honesty directly as a real, practical intervention against addiction (small daily dishonesties, she notes, are themselves a real, common human default — most adults tell one to two lies per day), and the conversation closes on real, unscripted warmth — Lembke's own direct, spontaneous praise for Nate's own intellectual fearlessness in confronting where current trends might lead, offered without prompting as the episode's own final substantive note before Nate's real, distinctive sign-off, 'my new friend.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Wolfram Schultz (real, directly cited neuroscientist, foundational dopamine-reward research); Nora Volkow (real, directly cited NIDA director, methamphetamine/dopamine research); Peter Whybrow (American Mania, real TGS episode directly cross-referenced, not built)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-193 (Anastassia Makarieva), from a complete
# real transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Independently
# cited by RR-21, Craig Tindale (TGS-207), Ove Hoegh-Guldberg (TGS-197),
# and Andrew Millison (TGS-178) before this episode itself existed
# here — resolves a real Concept.BioticPump gap explicitly flagged in
# Millison's own scopeNote. Real, honest correction: the raw
# auto-generated transcript repeatedly mis-renders her name as
# "Anastasia Eva" and her co-developer's as "Victor Goroff" — both
# corrected here to their real spellings, Makarieva and Gorshkov.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_193_AnastassiaMakarieva a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why We Need Forests: Their Vital Role in Climate Dynamics, Rain, and The Biotic Pump"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts physicist Anastassia Makarieva, co-developer of the biotic pump theory, opening with her own real, pointed methodological critique of climate science: models are calibrated using the world's most disturbed, degraded ecosystems — analogous, in her own real, vivid framing, to calibrating a healthy human baseline using only hospital patients who can 'at best survive till the next meeting with the doctor.' Argues genuinely intact, undisturbed forests — largely unstudied by comparison — actively regulate rainfall and climate stability, rather than merely responding passively to it."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.AnastassiaMakarieva ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/193-anastassia-makarieva> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GWdXCqVOFkY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/TGS-193-Anastassia-Makarieva-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.BioticPump ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_178_AndrewMillison ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, striking geographic evidence anchors the theory directly: the Amazon rainforest generates its own real, documented wet season before large-scale monsoon circulation would otherwise produce one, and continues significant dry-season transpiration — real, direct evidence forests actively drive rather than merely receive precipitation. A real, extended, personally significant closing section traces flying-river moisture transport from Siberia's own real taiga forests through to Russia's White Sea and Yenisei River systems, tied directly to Makarieva's own real, current concern about Russian timber exports to China accelerating deforestation in exactly the ecosystems her own research shows regulate regional and even hemispheric climate stability. A real, striking extension of Victor Gorshkov's own original size-limitation principle closes the conversation: Makarieva directly applies 'enshittification' — a real, popular internet-coined term for platforms degrading once they capture users — as a genuine cross-domain parallel, arguing large, inefficient systems (corporations, or oversized organisms in a degraded ecosystem) structurally lose their responsiveness to the external environment they depend on, precisely because their internal-to-external surface ratio shrinks as they grow. Closes on real, direct, unresolved urgency around international cooperation: asked for closing words, Makarieva's own real answer moves past forests specifically to a broader, direct appeal — 'we need to listen to each other,' naming national origin as a real, common but illegitimate basis for treating people as adversaries rather than potential collaborators."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Thomas Crowther (real TGS episode #177, tree-density mapping researcher, directly cross-referenced, not yet built); Carlos Nobre (real TGS episode #150, independently reconfirmed here — already known as deferred elsewhere in this graph, e.g. via Andrew Millison's TGS_178); Antonio Nobre (a real, distinct Amazon researcher — not to be confused with Carlos Nobre — directly cited for his own real 'flying rivers' work, not built)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-134 (Johan Rockström), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Independently cited
# by Ove Hoegh-Guldberg (TGS-197) and Pella Thiel (TGS-121) before this
# episode itself existed here. Resolves a genuinely surprising gap —
# no Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries existed despite the framework being
# referenced throughout this graph.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Johan Rockström: \"Planetary Boundaries: Exceeding Earth's Safe Limits\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Johan Rockström, real, direct originator of the Planetary Boundaries framework, opening with a real, striking structural point: even complete fossil fuel phase-out alone would still breach the 1.5°C climate boundary if humanity fails to also return biosphere health to a safe operating space, since intact land ecosystems currently absorb roughly 30% of fossil-fuel CO2 emissions — a real, direct 'shooting ourselves in the foot' dynamic where degrading nature simultaneously worsens the crisis and weakens the planet's own buffering capacity against it."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-06-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohanRockstrom ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/134-johan-rockstrom> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JaboF3vAsZs> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/TGS134JohanRockstromTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, striking regional case study grounds the abstract framework concretely: the Arctic is warming roughly three times faster than the rest of the planet, hosting 6 of 16 known real tipping-point systems, with Greenland ice-sheet melt directly affecting European temperature regulation via AMOC (Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation) dynamics. Rockström is real and direct about a genuine economic reframing closing the policy section: real carbon pricing at roughly $100/tonne is offered not as a punitive cost but as internalizing an externality already being paid involuntarily by the biosphere — paired directly with the real, current fact that coal is now more expensive than renewables even without subsidies, undercutting the common assumption that climate action requires economic sacrifice. Real, considered hope closes the episode without minimizing the real scale of the crisis: Rockström, who directly describes living in sobering climate data 'every day' as his own professional 'dark room,' credits real, direct interaction with change-makers worldwide for genuine optimism, citing real historical precedent that societal transitions are driven by committed minorities tipping an inert majority, not universal buy-in — noting real, current double-digit penetration of sustainability awareness and green-voting willingness across many societies as evidence of approaching exactly that kind of tipping point."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Levke Caesar (AMOC researcher, directly cited — already known to this graph as a deliberately deferred figure, matching the same scope decision made for Leon Simons in the Brett KenCairn build); Stefan Rahmstorf (real, directly cited climate scientist, not built); Ottmar Edenhofer (real, directly cited economist, carbon pricing, not built)."@en ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-31 — TGS-150 (Carlos Nobre), from a complete real
# transcript + show notes uploaded by MJSullivan. Independently cited
# by Andrew Millison (TGS-178) and Anastassia Makarieva (TGS-193)
# before this episode itself existed here.
#############################################

tgs:Interview.TGS_150_CarlosNobre a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carlos Nobre: \"Carbon Sink to Carbon Source? How the Amazon Rainforest Could Become a Self-Drying Savanna\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts Earth scientist Carlos Nobre, co-chair of the Science Panel for the Amazon, to discuss the real, specific mechanics of Amazon tipping-point risk: if the forest exceeds its critical threshold within the next 30-50 years, Nobre estimates a real loss of 50-70% of total forest cover, with that tipping point itself possibly reached as early as 2050 under current deforestation and warming trajectories. Real, striking mechanism grounds the risk directly: the rainforest canopy absorbs roughly 92% of incoming sunlight, and the forest actively recycles its own rainfall through transpiration — meaning sustained deforestation doesn't just remove trees, it can structurally collapse the water cycle the remaining forest depends on, converting rainforest to savanna."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-25"^^xsd:date ;
